{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md'","aggregated_output":"# dao.stream.host-boundary: dao.stream as the only host-dependency boundary\n\nEvery contact between the portable core and a host is either a `dao.stream`\nimplementation's host seam (world facts and effects) or one entry of a\nsmall, enumerable primitive profile with a portable oracle (machine-internal\ncarriers and pure functions). Porting a host is then: implement the stream\nseams the composition selects, implement the primitive profile against the\nfrozen vectors, write a `main`. Nothing else.\n\nStatus: design only; nothing commissioned. Written 2026-10-20 by the\nArchitect (claude-fable-5-1; rationale in\n`collab/1792495909000-architect-host-boundary.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\nNo code was changed; every claim about the tree is labelled. **Because this\ndocument was written by a Claude model, a non-Claude architect must sign it\noff before anything is commissioned from it or relied on** (the repo's\nstanding rule for Claude-authored work; §12).\n\nOwner direction (verbatim; the \"maybe\" is the owner's, so this is a design\nto evaluate, not a commission): \"maybe this is an opportunity to stop and\nrefactor so that the dao.stream is the boundary that crosses over host\nspecific dependency. once that is done, the only thing needs porting is\ndao.stream implementations\".\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) `:43-48` (all IO is a stream; no\n  function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary) and `:70` (a host\n  boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add\n  contracts).\n- [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md): taxonomy (§2), custody (§3),\n  the floor (§5), effects out and events in (§6), adapter discipline (§7).\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the contract (seven operations, outcome\n  maps, no waiting, no hidden global state).\n- [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md): the wasm import surface is one\n  implementation of the abstraction; no second ABI.\n- [dao.stream.codec.md](./dao.stream.codec.md): the transport codec track\n  (another orchestrator is implementing it; this document builds on it and\n  duplicates none of it).\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5: the conformance method.\n- [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md) and [dao.jing.cbor.md](./dao.jing.cbor.md):\n  Jing is passive syntax; the canonical byte corpus is the oracle.\n\nLabels: V = verified by reading this tree on 2026-10-20 (line cites); R =\nread or inferred from a report or log, not run; P = proposed, does not exist\nyet. Nothing was executed.\n\nThis document is standalone. A future host lane (a native Clojure dialect on\na Scheme runtime, a Rust peer, a wasm instance) is an example consumer of it,\nnever a dependency of it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem\n\nV: the portable core (`src/cljc`, 183 files) reaches hosts in two different\nways and the doctrine sanctions only one of them.\n\nThe sanctioned way is already the pattern for transports: a portable core\nunder `src/cljc/dao/stream/` plus a per-host seam under `src/clj`, `src/cljs`,\n`src/cljd` (`ws/jvm.clj` 471 lines, `ws/node.cljs` 340, `ws/dart.cljd` 339,\n`ws/browser.cljs` 112; `datagram/jvm.clj` 111, `datagram/node.cljs` 110,\n`datagram/dart.cljd` 173; `waitset/driver.*` 49/80/79). The seam is a plain\nmap of functions the composition hands in (`yin.repl.host/websocket`,\n`src/cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc:20-37`, returns `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}`;\n`dao.stream.datagram.jvm/bind!`, `jvm.clj:22-34`, takes a deposit writer and\nreturns `{:send! :close!}`). The portable core never names a socket library.\n\nThe other way is reader conditionals inside portable files that reach a host\nlibrary directly. V, counted by `rg -c '#\\?'` over `src/cljc` (every\nconditional, including the harmless `catch` arms):\n\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| File                              | `#?`  | What the arms reach (V)                                                                                 |\n+===================================+=======+=========================================================================================================+\n| `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` (1562 lines) | 52    | `BigInteger`/JS `BigInt`/Dart `BigInt` helpers (`:106-254`), float bit extraction (`:525-560`,          |\n|                                   |       | `Double/doubleToRawLongBits`, `js/DataView`, `ByteData`), the wire writer namespace (`:58-60`: Boring   |\n|                                   |       | on JVM and JS, the `cbor` Dart package behind `dao.jing.cbor.cljd`).                                    |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/data/btree.cljc` (2158)      | 35    | Host arrays and `^long` hints (via `dao.data.arrays`, 21 arms; `Arrays/parallelSort` at `:145`).       |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (299)  | 30    | The declared integer shim: carrier recognition, promote/demote, 13 big-integer operations               |\n|                                   |       | (`:1-28` header table: JVM `long`/`BigInt`, JS `Number`/`BigInt`, Dart `int`/`BigInt`).                |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/jing/file.cljc` (455)        | 19    | `java.io.RandomAccessFile`, Node `fs`, `dart:io RandomAccessFile`: open, length, read-at, write,        |\n|                                   |       | truncate, sync, close (`:163-259`).                                                                     |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/space/store/fs.cljc` (494)   | 17    | Exclusive directory lock (`FileChannel.tryLock`, Dart `lockSync`, Node claim files), temp-write +       |\n|                                   |       | fsync + rename, directory sync (`:77-82, :198-210, :292-314, :352-378, :419-448`).                      |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc` (132) | 14    | Every form `:cljd nil`-gated; wraps `boring.core/encode` (JVM and JS only).                            |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (274)   | 16    | Ed25519 via `java.security`, `ed25519_edwards` (Dart), `goog.crypt` plus a JS library; `SecureRandom` |\n|                                   |       | for key generation (`:8-16, :55-66, :79, :120`).                                                        |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/jing.cljc` (629)             | 12    | BLAKE3 (`io.github.rctcwyvrn.blake3`, `@noble/hashes`, `blake3_dart`) and SHA-256                       |\n|                                   |       | (`MessageDigest`, `goog.crypt.Sha256`, `package:crypto`) (`:32-40, :59-135`).                           |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc` (16),      | 16+11 | Bytes predicate, char access, NFC normalization (`java.text.Normalizer`, `unorm_dart`), float64 bits,   |\n| `dao/space/store.cljc`            |       | `int?`/`Double` carrier tests; `dart:io` directory listing and EDN reading in the store.                |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yin/repl/main.cljc` (15),        | 15+15 | `System/currentTimeMillis`, `js/Date.now`, `Thread.` pollers (`main.cljc:1242-1435`): the per-host      |\n| `yin/repl.cljc`                   |       | `main`, not the core; `repl.cljc` is almost all `catch` arms plus `System/getProperty` (`:275`).         |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/stream/codec.cljc` (4),      | 4+4+5 | The host number predicate and host-bytes predicate (`codec.cljc:19,45`); base64 (`goog.crypt.base64`,   |\n| `dao/stream/base64.cljc`,         |       | `dart:convert`); `Uint8List` length/alloc/slice/copy (`chunks.cljc:8-32`).                              |\n| `dao/stream/chunks.cljc`          |       |                                                                                                         |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/jing/dht.cljc` (10),         | 10+7  | Bytes helpers (`dht.cljc:100-121`); `SecureRandom`/`getRandomValues`/`Random.secure` for node           |\n| `dao/space/dht.cljc`              |       | identity and nonces (`space/dht.cljc:145-151`); the datagram host seam selected by reader arm (`:137`). |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao/jing/content/async.cljc` (3) | 3     | A timer per host (`java.util.concurrent` delayed executor, `js/setTimeout`, `dart:async Timer`,         |\n|                                   |       | `:47-58`): a scheduler inside a portable namespace.                                                     |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc`   | 6     | The generated ANTLR recognizers, one per host, and `yang.antlr.cst` (`src/clj` 221, `src/cljs` 200,    |\n| (6)                               |       | `src/cljd` 203 lines), the host walk over the ANTLR runtime.                                            |\n+-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nR (a spike report that brought the core up on a fourth host): 86 of the 91\nnamespaces in the `yin.repl` closure loaded unchanged; the failures were\nexactly the rows above (integer shim, Jing hashing, the Boring writer, the\nfile and store namespaces, `parallelSort`, the Java ANTLR parser). So the\nproblem is not breadth: it is that a dozen files each carry their own private\nhost contract, with no shared enumeration, no shared oracle and, for some\n(the big-integer helpers), two copies.\n\nV, the duplication: `yin.vm.integer.host` declares that it \"depends on\nnothing: it does not reach into `dao.jing.cbor`'s private big-integer\nhelpers\" (`host.cljc:23-25`), and `dao.jing.cbor` keeps its own eight\n(`cbor.cljc:112-216`). Two shims, two sets of host arms, one contract.\n\n## 2. Doctrine applied: where the line is\n\nThe doctrine draws one line, and it is not \"every host call is a stream\".\n\n- **The floor** (`datom.world.abi.md` §5): \"The floor is the need to obtain\n  world facts that shipped code cannot compute. That seam is immovable; the\n  engineering frontier is movable: machine-internal operations can move\n  inside the instance without changing the abstraction.\" §4: \"Machine-internal\n  work executes inside the wasm instance ... Only effects/facts cross through\n  the stream adapters.\"\n- **Custody** (§3): in-machine values are a superset of what crosses; host\n  types never cross. The in-machine value model is the host's own (a `long`,\n  a `BigInt`, a byte array); it is below the boundary by definition.\n- **Effects out, events in** (§6, §7): world contact is an appended effect\n  value and a deposited event; an adapter is a map of transform functions\n  handed the deposit operation and its own host resource.\n\nSo a host dependency is one of two kinds, and the design treats them\ndifferently:\n\n+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Kind                           | Definition                                                         | Target shape                                                     |\n+================================+====================================================================+==================================================================+\n| **World fact or effect**       | Something shipped code cannot compute: a socket, a file, a lock, a | A `dao.stream` implementation: portable core plus a host seam    |\n|                                | clock reading, entropy, another process's parser output.           | value the composition supplies (§4). The seam is the only host   |\n|                                |                                                                    | code. This is the owner's \"only dao.stream implementations\".     |\n+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Machine-internal carrier or  | A value kind the dialect itself defines (exact integer, float,     | One **primitive profile**: an enumerable set of functions with a |\n| pure function over carriers**  | string, byte array, host array) or a deterministic function over   | portable oracle (frozen vectors or corpora) per entry (§4.3).    |\n|                                | such values (hashing, signing, canonical encoding, NFC).           | Never a stream: a stream round trip per integer addition is not  |\n|                                |                                                                    | an abstraction, it is a 10^3-10^5 slowdown of the inner loop.    |\n+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**The hot-path line, stated.** Anything invoked per VM step, per guest\nvalue, per encoded byte or per content address is machine-internal and stays\na function. Anything invoked per I/O event (an open, a bind, a lock, a\nreceived datagram, a parsed source unit) is a stream operation. The\nobservable test: if the operation's answer could be different on a different\nmachine at a different time, it is a world fact and crosses a stream; if its\nanswer is a function of its inputs alone, it is a primitive and has an oracle.\n\nThis refines, and does not contradict, `datom.world.md:70`. A primitive\nprofile adds no contract to `dao.stream` and is not a second ABI: it is the\ndialect's value model made explicit and testable, which `datom.world.abi.md`\n§3 already presupposes (\"in-machine values\"). What the owner's sentence\nachieves under this reading: **a port implements stream seams; the primitive\nprofile is the port's value model, verified by vectors, not designed per\nhost.**\n\n## 3. Classification of every kind of host dependency\n\nFor each kind: where it lives today (V), the answer, (a) portable, (b)\nstream, (c) seam, and why.\n\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Kind                          | Today (V)                                                      | Ans | Why, and the cost                                               |\n+===============================+================================================================+=====+=================================================================+\n| Effects and I/O: sockets      | Already (b): `dao.stream.ws`, `.datagram`, `.udp` portable     | (b) | The pattern to copy. Unchanged by this design except that the   |\n| (WebSocket, datagram, UDP)    | cores with per-host seams (§1).                                |     | seam maps are named in the host contract (§4) and gain a shared |\n|                               |                                                                |     | implementation-conformance suite (§6).                          |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Effects and I/O: wake, sleep, | `dao.stream.waitset.driver` per host (seam);                   | (c) | Sleep machinery is the driver's, not the core's                 |\n| schedule                      | `dao.jing.content` `.async/default-schedule` **inside** a      | ->  | (`waitset/driver.clj:1-14`). The one portable-file timer moves  |\n|                               | portable file (`:47-58`).                                      | drv | to a composition parameter: the caller supplies `schedule`.     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | Zero hot-path cost; one callsite.                               |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Clock                         | `yin.repl.main` (per-host main, `:1242-1435`); `dao.stream.ws` | drv | A clock reading is a world fact. It is already passed in as     |\n|                               | `accept-connection!` takes `now` as an argument                |     | data by the driver; the core holds no clock. Keep; the host     |\n|                               | (`ws.cljc:639`).                                               |     | contract lists \"a monotonic and a wall reading\" as `main`'s     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | duty.                                                           |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Entropy                       | `dao.space.dht` `:145-151` (`SecureRandom`, `getRandomValues`, | drv | Entropy is a world fact and a custody matter: the core never    |\n|                               | `Random.secure`); `yin.vm.linker.sign` `:79,:120` (key seeds). |     | reads the OS. Both sites take `random-bytes` as a composition   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | parameter (a function `n -> bytes`), supplied by `main`.        |\n|                               |                                                                |     | Per-event frequency; no hot path.                               |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Threads                       | Inside seams only: datagram receiver thread                    | drv | Already outside the core. The contract states the rule: a seam  |\n|                               | (`datagram/jvm.clj`), `yin.repl.main` pollers; the core is     |     | may use threads to deposit; the core is never entered from one  |\n|                               | single-owner by design.                                        |     | (`abi.md` §6, events in).                                       |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Exact integers and bit        | `yin.vm.integer.host` (the shim, 299 lines, 13 operations)     | (c) | A carrier. Every present host has a native big integer, and the |\n| operations                    | under `yin.vm.integer` kernels (913 lines, written once over   |     | kernels already put the algorithms in portable code (floor      |\n|                               | the shim); a second private copy in `dao.jing.cbor:106-254`.   |     | division, shifts without building 2^k, bit length, float text). |\n|                               |                                                                |     | (a) pure limbs would be a 10x-100x regression on `integer/add`, |\n|                               |                                                                |     | a guest-module hot path, on three hosts that have native        |\n|                               |                                                                |     | bigints, and the owner's guidance is libraries over             |\n|                               |                                                                |     | hand-rolling. (b) is excluded by the hot-path line. The move:   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | one shim, under `dao`, used by both `yin.vm.integer` and        |\n|                               |                                                                |     | `dao.jing.cbor`; the existing `integer_v3_fixtures` and the     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | cbor corpus are its oracle. A host whose native bit operations  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | are wrong above 2^63 implements the shim's bit entries          |\n|                               |                                                                |     | arithmetically; that is allowed by the shim and caught by the   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | fixtures.                                                       |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Floats: bits, NaN, infinity   | `integer/host.cljc:86-108`; `cbor.cljc:525-560`;               | (c) | Carrier. Two entries (`float64->bits`, `bits->float64`) plus    |\n|                               | `debruijn.cljc:855-885`; `yin.vm.data:77-92`.                  |     | three predicates. Four copies today; one after.                 |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Byte arrays: alloc, length,   | `dao.stream.chunks:8-32`, `dao.jing.dht:100-121`,              | (c) | Carrier (`byte[]`, `Uint8Array`, `Uint8List`). About eight      |\n| get, slice, copy, equality    | `yin.vm.linker:1232`, `sign.cljc:55-59`, `codec.cljc:45`,      |     | entries. Five copies today; one after. The predicate            |\n|                               | `cbor/boring.cljc:46-51`, `datagram.cljc:63-66`.               |     | `host-bytes?` that the codec track placed in `dao.stream.codec` |\n|                               |                                                                |     | (C0, V) reads the same seam; nothing in the codec track moves.  |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Strings: UTF-16 units, UTF-8  | `yin.vm.data:119-173`, `debruijn.cljc:241,319` (NFC),          | (c) | Carrier. Code-unit access, UTF-8 encode/decode, NFC. The NFC    |\n| bytes, NFC                    | `jing.cljc:65-71,131-135` (UTF-8), `sign.cljc:62-66`,          |     | entry is a pure function with a library per host and the        |\n|                               | `base64.cljc` (base64 over bytes).                             |     | Unicode vectors as oracle; (a) a portable NFC table is possible |\n|                               |                                                                |     | but is a library-sized job for no gain. Base64 is (a): 40 lines |\n|                               |                                                                |     | of portable code over the bytes seam replaces three library     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | arms.                                                           |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Host arrays (`dao.data`       | `dao.data.arrays` (187 lines, 21 arms); `parallelSort` at      | (c) | Carrier for one subsystem's node storage. Keep the namespace as |\n| `.btree` node storage)        | `:145`.                                                        |     | the seam it already is; replace `parallelSort` with a stable    |\n|                               |                                                                |     | sort entry whose result order is the oracle (the btree          |\n|                               |                                                                |     | fixtures). Required only by compositions that use the durable   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | index.                                                          |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Hashing (BLAKE3, SHA-256)     | `dao.jing:59-135`, three libraries per algorithm.              | (c) | A pure function over bytes, invoked per content address (every  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | `materialize!`, every seal, every H/R): hot. Not a world fact,  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | so not (b). Not (a): owner guidance, and a portable BLAKE3 over |\n|                               |                                                                |     | the integer shim would be slow where speed matters most. Seam   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | of two entries, `blake3-bytes` and `sha256-bytes`, with the     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | official test vectors as oracle; the string forms become        |\n|                               |                                                                |     | portable (UTF-8 seam then bytes).                               |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Signatures (Ed25519)          | `yin.vm.linker.sign`, three libraries; message assembly and    | (c) | `verify` is pure and per admitted module; `sign` is pure given  |\n|                               | hex are already portable (`:19-70`).                           |     | the seed. Seam of three entries (`sign`, `verify`,              |\n|                               |                                                                |     | `keypair-from-seed`) over bytes, RFC 8032 and                   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | `sign_vectors.edn` as oracle. A signer whose key never enters   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | the process (an HSM, another peer) is a legitimate **stream**   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | composition later (request bytes, answer signature); custody    |\n|                               |                                                                |     | (§3) already allows it. Not designed here.                      |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Canonical content codec (Jing | `dao.jing.cbor`: decode is **already portable** (its own       | (a) | Jing is syntax and must be byte-identical everywhere; the       |\n| CBOR writer)                  | structural reader, `:29-35`); encode hands a wire tree to      |     | strongest guarantee is one writer. The writer is small: a wire  |\n|                               | Boring (JVM, JS) or the `cbor` Dart package (`cbor/cljd.cljd`, |     | tree of nulls, booleans, ints, bytes, text, arrays,             |\n|                               | 107 lines). Boring is itself a Clojure library (R).            |     | bytewise-sorted maps and tags, over the bytes seam. The frozen  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | corpus `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and               |\n|                               |                                                                |     | `cbor_fixtures` are a byte-level oracle. Removes two host       |\n|                               |                                                                |     | libraries from the Jing path and the Dart wire file. Perf:      |\n|                               |                                                                |     | Boring is Clojure too; no regression argument, to be measured   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | in the slice (§8 H4).                                           |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Transport codecs (Transit,    | The codec track (`dao.stream.codec.md`); C0 and C1 landed (V:  | --  | Owned there. After its C3, no shared namespace requires Transit |\n| CBOR on the wire)             | `git log` `8351e639`, `eacaed90`).                             |     | and a host selects the codec it implements (`codec.md` §6.3).   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | This document adds nothing and touches none of its files (§8    |\n|                               |                                                                |     | ordering).                                                      |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Durable storage: content log, | `dao.jing.file` (random-access file), `dao.space.store.fs`     | (b) | World facts (durability, exclusivity). The stream is **already  |\n| directory lock, HEAD replace  | (lock, temp+fsync+rename, dir sync), `dao.space.store`,        | +   | there**: `dao.stream.journal` is a dao.stream over              |\n|                               | `journal/file`, `btree/storage`: 56 arms across five portable  | (c) | `dao.jing.file` under the `store.fs` lock                       |\n|                               | files.                                                         |     | (`journal/file.cljc:1-23`). What is missing is the seam         |\n|                               |                                                                |     | **beneath** it: the host calls are inlined as reader arms       |\n|                               |                                                                |     | instead of handed in as one value, as the socket seams are.     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | Target: one durable-storage seam value (§4.2) supplied by the   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | composition; `dao.jing.file`, `store.fs` and `store` become     |\n|                               |                                                                |     | portable over it. Per-I/O-event frequency; framing, recovery,   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | lock discipline and the HEAD protocol stay portable and tested  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | once. The content file's \"not a stream\" decision (`file.cljc`   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | docstring, Decision 2) is unchanged: the file is the journal's  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | backend, and the journal is the stream.                         |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Foreign-language parser       | `yang.python.antlr.parser` \"reads sealed source units from one | (b) | Already stream-shaped: the parser is a **stage** between two    |\n| (Python frontend)             | stream and writes CST packets to another\" (`parser.cljc:1-7,   |     | streams and \"no ANTLR object leaves\" the host walk              |\n|                               | 65-75`); packets are plain data (`yang.antlr.packet`); the     |     | (`cst.clj:14`). A host either implements the stage (an ANTLR    |\n|                               | host walk is per host (`yang.antlr.cst` x3) plus generated     |     | target for its language) or **runs the stage elsewhere**: the   |\n|                               | recognizers.                                                   |     | CST packet stream is the same data over any `dao.stream`        |\n|                               |                                                                |     | implementation. Per source unit; a stream round trip is cheap.  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | The design names the stage's host adapter as a seam value and   |\n|                               |                                                                |     | makes the remote-stage composition a tested option (§8 H7); no  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | second packet format.                                           |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Error and type reflection     | `catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` in ~40  | --  | Dialect syntax, not a dependency; no host library. Out of       |\n|                               | files; `ex-message`; `(.-runtimeType x)` for diagnostics.      |     | scope. A `dao.host.error/catch-all` macro could remove the      |\n|                               |                                                                |     | repetition; listed in §10 as a non-goal unless the owner wants  |\n|                               |                                                                |     | it.                                                             |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| GUI, graphics, demos          | `dao.gui.*`, `dao.postgraphics.*`, `datomworld.demo.*` (about  | --  | Not part of the `yin.repl` core closure; each is a composition  |\n|                               | 60 arms).                                                      |     | with its own host seam. Out of scope; §10 OQ1.                  |\n+-------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n## 4. The host-implementation contract\n\nA host implements three things. The set is small because each item is a\nclosed list in this document; adding to a list is a contract change\nreviewed here, never a per-host decision.\n\n### 4.1 Stream implementations and their seams (world facts)\n\nA `dao.stream` implementation is a portable namespace plus, where it touches\nthe world, a **seam value**: a plain map of functions the composition\nconstructs on the host and passes in (`abi.md` §7: an adapter is a value, not\na namespace; which stream it deposits to is the caller's composition). The\nin-memory implementations need no seam and no host code.\n\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| Implementation             | Portable core (V)                              | Seam value a host supplies (V today, P as       | Needed by                              |\n|                            |                                                | named)                                          |                                        |\n+============================+================================================+=================================================+========================================+\n| Ring buffer, memory log,   | `dao.stream.ringbuffer`, `.memory_log`,        | None.                                           | Everything. A pure evaluator host      |\n| remote channel, waitset,   | `.remote_channel`, `.waitset`, `.observe`,     |                                                 | stops here.                            |\n| middleware, apply          | `.middleware`, `.apply`                        |                                                 |                                        |\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| WebSocket                  | `dao.stream.ws` (1 arm, the bytes predicate)   | `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}` (`yin.repl.host`  | Network REPL, linker transfer, DHT     |\n|                            |                                                | `:20-33`); `:connect!` returns `{:send!         | head publication.                      |\n|                            |                                                | :close!}`.                                      |                                        |\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| Datagram and UDP           | `dao.stream.datagram` (4 arms, bytes),         | `bind!` taking `{:identity :deposit :bind-host  | DHT, discovery.                        |\n|                            | `dao.stream.udp`                               | :bind-port :max-bytes}`, returning `{:send!     |                                        |\n|                            |                                                | :close!}` (`datagram.cljc:15-23`).              |                                        |\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| Durable journal (and the   | `dao.stream.journal`, `dao.jing.file`,         | P, §4.2: `{:open! :length :read-at :write-at    | Durable index store, transactor        |\n| content file and store     | `dao.space.store.fs`, `dao.space.store`,       | :truncate! :sync! :close! :lock! :unlock!       | ledger, content store on disk.         |\n| under it)                  | `journal.file` (56 arms today)                 | :replace-atomically! :sync-dir! :list-dir       |                                        |\n|                            |                                                | :read-text}`.                                   |                                        |\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| Parser stage               | `yang.antlr.export`, `.packet`,                | P: a grammar profile `{:grammar-id :make-lexer  | Python frontend. Optional: the stage   |\n|                            | `yang.python.antlr.*`                          | :make-parser :start}` (`cst.clj:9-12`, V) plus  | may run on another host over any       |\n|                            |                                                | the host walk, declared as one seam value.      | stream implementation (§3).            |\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n| wasm import surface        | `dao.stream.wasm.md` §1: seven imports.        | The imports themselves. The same contract;      | wasm instances.                        |\n|                            |                                                | nothing here changes it.                        |                                        |\n+----------------------------+------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+\n\nRule for every seam (restating `abi.md` §6-§7 as acceptance): a seam entry\ntakes host arguments and returns plain data or deposits plain data; it never\ninvokes application code; the host's error object is classified into data\ninside the seam; a seam that cannot do something answers a qualified\nunsupported outcome, never a throw into the core. The durability declaration\npattern (`journal/file.cljc:29-41`, a map of facts the backend answers) is\nthe model for every seam's capability statement.\n\n### 4.2 The durable-storage seam (P)\n\nThe one new seam. Today's arms, read as a contract:\n\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| Entry                   | Today (V)                                                                        | Outcome data                                         |\n+=========================+==================================================================================+======================================================+\n| `open!` path mode       | `file.cljc:163-175` (`RandomAccessFile \"rw\"`, Node `fs.openSync`, Dart           | handle, or `{:unsupported}` / `{:failed reason}`     |\n|                         | `openSync`)                                                                      |                                                      |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| `length`, `read-at`,    | `file.cljc:183-259`: seek+readInt/readFully, setLength, write+sync, close        | bytes or int; `:sync!` answers `{:synced}` or        |\n| `write-at!`,            |                                                                                  | `{:failed}`; never raises into framing code          |\n| `truncate!`, `sync!`,   |                                                                                  |                                                      |\n| `close!`                |                                                                                  |                                                      |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| `lock!` dir, `unlock!`  | `store/fs.cljc:198-210` (`tryLock`, `lockSync`, claim files)                     | `{:held token}` / `{:refused holder}`; the seam      |\n|                         |                                                                                  | declares its `lock-kind` (`journal/file.cljc:38-40`) |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| `replace-atomically!`   | `store/fs.cljc:352-378` (temp write, fsync, rename), `sync-dir!` `:292-314`,     | `{:replaced}` / `{:failed}`; `directory-sync-        |\n| `sync-dir!`             | `directory-sync-supported?` `:265`                                               | supported?` becomes a declared capability            |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| `list-dir`, `read-text`,| `store.cljc:105,159`, `store/fs.cljc:419-448`, `journal/file.cljc:55-61`         | data                                                 |\n| `ensure-dir!`           |                                                                                  |                                                      |\n+-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n\nWhat stays portable and is written once: framing and torn-tail recovery\n(`file.cljc` docstring, F1-F6), the lock protocol including Node's claim-file\nliveness rule (`store/fs.cljc:9-29`), the HEAD replace protocol, replay\norder. These are the parts with the bugs; a port gets them for free.\n\n### 4.3 The primitive profile (machine-internal)\n\nOne namespace family, `dao.host.*` (P; the name is OQ2), each a `.cljc` file\nwhose only content is reader-conditional entries and a docstring stating the\noracle. Every other portable file requires these and carries **no** host arm\nof its own (enforced by the grep gate, §8 H0).\n\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| Namespace (P)         | Entries (consolidated from, V)                                  | Count   | Oracle (V where it exists)                              |\n+=======================+=================================================================+=========+=========================================================+\n| `dao.host.integer`    | `yin.vm.integer.host` entire (recognition x3, float predicates  | ~20     | `test/yin/vm/integer_v3_fixtures.cljc` and               |\n|                       | x3, promote/demote/from-native x4, 13 big operations) absorbing |         | `integer_test.cljc` (26 deftests); the cbor corpus for  |\n|                       | `cbor.cljc:106-254` (`big`, `b+`, `b-`, `b*`, `bquot`, `bcmp`,  |         | >64-bit integers and magnitude bytes.                   |\n|                       | `bgcd`, `b->int`, `magnitude-bytes`, bit length).               |         |                                                         |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.float`      | `float64->bits`, `bits->float64`, `nan?`, `infinite?`,          | ~5      | cbor corpus float cases (`7ff8...` NaN rule); de Bruijn |\n|                       | `finite?` (from `cbor.cljc:525-560`, `debruijn.cljc:855-885`,   |         | scalar fixtures.                                        |\n|                       | `integer/host.cljc:86-108`, `data.cljc:77-92`).                 |         |                                                         |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.bytes`      | `bytes?`, `alloc`, `length`, `get`, `set!`, `slice`, `copy!`,   | ~9      | Property tests (round trip, slice laws) and the cbor    |\n|                       | `equal?`, `from-vector` (from `chunks`, `jing.dht`, `linker`,   |         | corpus (byte-string cases).                             |\n|                       | `sign`, `datagram`, `codec`, `cbor/boring`).                    |         |                                                         |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.text`       | `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`, `utf8-encode`,      | ~6      | `data_test` code-point cases; cbor corpus               |\n|                       | `utf8-decode`, `nfc` (from `data.cljc:119-173`, `debruijn:241,  |         | `unpaired-surrogate` refusals; Unicode NFC vectors for  |\n|                       | 319`, `jing.cljc`, `sign.cljc:62-66`).                          |         | `nfc`.                                                  |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.hash`       | `blake3-bytes`, `sha256-bytes` (from `jing.cljc:59-135`; string | 2       | Official BLAKE3 and SHA-256 vectors (the spike checked   |\n|                       | forms become portable over `text`).                             |         | three, R); `hash_registry_contract_test`; every Jing    |\n|                       |                                                                 |         | address in the corpus.                                  |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.sign`       | `keypair-from-seed`, `sign`, `verify` (from `sign.cljc`; hex,   | 3       | RFC 8032 vectors and `sign_vectors.edn` (V:              |\n|                       | domains, message assembly already portable).                    |         | `sign_test.cljc`, 5 deftests).                          |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| `dao.host.array`      | `dao.data.arrays` as is (`anew`, `aget`, `aset`, `alength`,     | ~10     | btree fixtures (`psset_fixtures`, `btree_test`); the    |\n|                       | `acopy`, `aclone`, `asort` made stable, ...).                   |         | sort entry's order is pinned by a fixture.              |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n| Portable, no seam     | base64 (over `bytes`), the Jing CBOR writer (over `bytes`,      | 0       | `base64` tests; `cbor-v1.json`, `cbor_fixtures`,        |\n|                       | `integer`, `float`, `text`), hex.                               |         | `cbor_conformance_test`.                                |\n+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n\nAbout 55 functions. Each entry's docstring states its law on every host (as\n`integer/host.cljc` does today, V), and the profile has one manifest test\nnamespace, `dao.host.conformance-test` (P), that runs every oracle above on\nevery lane. A host that cannot provide an entry natively implements it in\nportable code over the other entries (bit operations over arithmetic;\n`asort` as a mergesort); the oracle does not care how.\n\nLayering: `dao.host.*` requires nothing in the repo. `dao.jing` and\n`yin.vm.integer` require it; `yin.vm.integer.host` is deleted after its\nconsumers move (no backward compatibility is owed in this repo). Dependency\ndirection is unchanged: `dao` never requires `yin`.\n\n### 4.4 The driver (`main`)\n\nPer host, outside the core, and not a contract of this document beyond the\nlist of what it supplies: a clock (`now` as data), `random-bytes`, a\n`schedule`/wake source, construction of the seam values of §4.1, and the\nstdin/stdout or socket loop. `yin.repl.main` is that for the three hosts\ntoday (V, 15 arms, all in this category).\n\n### 4.5 Relation to the conformance kit and the wasm surface\n\n`yin.vm.engine.md` §E.5 is engine conformance: fixtures over images, B0\nnormalization, and (E.5.1) \"compare canonical stream-adapter traffic as well\nas full semantic traces\". It assumes the host value model and the stream\nadapters are correct; it does not test them. This document supplies the two\nsuites §E.5 presupposes:\n\n1. **Primitive-profile conformance** (`dao.host.conformance-test`): the\n   vectors of §4.3, per lane. E.5.2's gate \"R, owner, seal, profile and\n   manifest addresses are bit-identical\" is downstream of this suite: a hash\n   or integer divergence shows here first, with a named entry.\n2. **Stream-implementation conformance** (P, `dao.stream.conformance-test`):\n   for each implementation, the contract's exhaustive outcome tables\n   (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention) exercised through a scripted seam:\n   every declared outcome reachable, no outcome outside the set, no wait, no\n   throw, append-once-after-full, close idempotent, descriptor round trip.\n   E.5.3's W0 adapter probes (\"canonical request/outcome round trips, all\n   declared stream outcomes, handle ownership, ... full-before-append and no\n   mutation on rejected adapter calls\") are exactly this suite instantiated\n   for the wasm implementation. The design makes W0's list the shared list\n   and runs it against the socket and journal seams too.\n\nThe wasm import surface is unaffected: it is one implementation of §4.1 and\nits canonical bytes are Jing's (`dao.stream.wasm.md` §3). A portable Jing\nwriter (§3) makes the oracle for those bytes one implementation rather than\nthree, which strengthens E.5.2 without changing it.\n\n## 5. What porting a host consists of, after this design\n\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| #   | Step                                                                  | Size and proof                                                             |\n+=====+=======================================================================+============================================================================+\n| 1   | Choose carriers and implement `dao.host.*` (§4.3) for the dialect.    | ~55 functions; green `dao.host.conformance-test`. Hash and signature        |\n|     |                                                                       | entries bind a library (owner guidance); everything else is the dialect's  |\n|     |                                                                       | own arithmetic, strings and arrays.                                        |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2   | Load the portable core with a reader feature for the dialect only     | Zero source edits in `src/cljc` outside `dao.host.*`: the grep gate (§8    |\n|     | where the dialect's syntax requires it.                               | H0) is what proves this.                                                   |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3   | Run the pure-evaluator milestone: in-memory streams, local REPL.       | The existing `yin.vm` and `yin.repl` suites minus network and storage;    |\n|     |                                                                       | §E.5 engine fixtures.                                                      |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 4   | Implement the seam values the composition needs, one per milestone:   | Each ~100-350 lines (V: today's seams); green `dao.stream.conformance-test`|\n|     | durable storage (§4.2), datagram, WebSocket, parser stage.            | per implementation, then the existing cross-host peer suites.              |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 5   | Write `main` (§4.4).                                                  | Host-specific by nature; the REPL shell tests.                             |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nFor a native Clojure dialect on a Scheme runtime (the example a recent spike\nexercised, R), steps 1 and 2 are the whole difference from today's spike\noverlays: the four overlay files become one `dao.host.*` implementation with\nvectors, and the content writer needs no port because it is portable. For a\nRust peer, step 1 is the Rust value model (already planned in\n`yin.vm.rust.md`) and step 4 is its native `dao.stream` peer; this document\nadds the conformance suites it would run.\n\n## 6. Verifiability\n\n- **Byte-level oracles exist for the hot primitives**: the cbor-v1 corpus,\n  the integer v3 fixtures, the sign vectors, the hash registry test. The\n  design moves code behind them without changing a byte: every slice in §8\n  is acceptance-tested as \"identical corpus bytes and identical fixture\n  results on three lanes\".\n- **Enumerability is mechanical**: after H0 a grep over `src/cljc` for host\n  arms outside `dao/host/` and the named seam files is empty, and CI keeps it\n  empty.\n- **Seams are testable without hosts**: a scripted seam value (a map of\n  recording functions) drives the stream-implementation suite on every lane\n  without a socket or a disk, which is how E.5.1 already tests adapters\n  (\"scripted transports exist in every composition\").\n\n## 7. Migration principles\n\nExpand, migrate, contract (the codec track's sequence, `codec.md` §7.1, V):\nadd the new namespace, move consumers one at a time, delete the old arms.\nEach slice lands alone under the quick tier on three lanes with recorded\ntest and assertion counts; \"unchanged\" means equal counts plus any added\ntest named. Byte-identical acceptance wherever a corpus exists. The slow\ntier runs once at the end of phase A and once at the end of phase B (owner\ncadence: slow tier at phase boundaries).\n\n## 8. Slices, smallest first\n\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Phase  | Change                                                                         | Falsifiable acceptance                                            |\n+=======+========+================================================================================+===================================================================+\n| H0    | freeze | No `src/` change. A committed allowlist of files permitted to contain host     | The gate passes on the current tree with exactly today's list;   |\n|       |        | arms (today's §1 table) and a test that fails when any other `src/cljc` file   | it fails when a `#?(:clj` is added to a file outside the list     |\n|       |        | gains one. Record per-lane counts. Record the Jing corpus hash set.            | (one negative test). Counts recorded.                             |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H1    | expand | Create `dao.host.integer`, `dao.host.float`, `dao.host.bytes`, `dao.host.text` | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus `dao.host.conformance-   |\n|       |        | with today's arms moved verbatim, plus `dao.host.conformance-test` running the | test` named; cbor corpus bytes identical; `integer_v3_fixtures`   |\n|       |        | existing oracles against them. No consumer changes.                            | unchanged. Allowlist grows by the four files.                     |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H2    | migr.  | `yin.vm.integer` and `dao.jing.cbor` require `dao.host.integer`/`float`;       | Green; counts unchanged; corpus identical; `grep BigInteger       |\n|       |        | `cbor.cljc:106-254` and `yin.vm.integer.host` deleted. `chunks`, `jing.dht`,   | src/cljc` hits only `dao/host/integer.cljc`; allowlist shrinks by |\n|       |        | `linker`, `sign`, `datagram`, `codec`, `data`, `debruijn` read `bytes`/`text`. | the migrated files.                                               |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H3    | expand | `dao.host.hash` and `dao.host.sign`; `dao.jing` and `yin.vm.linker.sign`       | Green; counts unchanged plus the vector tests named; every Jing   |\n|       | +migr. | require them; their arms deleted. Base64 made portable over `bytes`.           | address in the corpus identical; `sign_vectors.edn` passes;       |\n|       |        |                                                                                | `grep MessageDigest\\|Blake3\\|noble\\|ed25519 src/cljc` hits only   |\n|       |        |                                                                                | `dao/host/`.                                                      |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H4    | migr.  | Portable Jing CBOR writer over `dao.host.*`; `dao.jing.cbor` requires it on    | Green; `cbor-v1.json`, `cbor_fixtures`, `cbor_conformance_test`   |\n|       |        | every host; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` and `dao.jing.cbor.cljd` deleted; the       | byte-identical on three lanes; the frozen DHT and linker image    |\n|       |        | Boring dependency stays only for `dao.stream.cbor.boring` (codec track's).     | goldens unchanged; a write-throughput measurement recorded        |\n|       |        |                                                                                | against Boring on the JVM (informational; not a gate).            |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H5    | migr.  | Entropy and scheduler to composition parameters: `dao.space.dht` and           | Green; counts unchanged; `grep SecureRandom\\|getRandomValues\\|    |\n|       |        | `yin.vm.linker.sign` take `random-bytes`; `dao.jing.content.async` takes       | setTimeout src/cljc` hits only `yin/repl/main.cljc` (the driver)  |\n|       |        | `schedule`; `yin.repl.main` supplies them.                                     | or nothing.                                                       |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|       |        | **Phase A boundary: slow tier once. Everything above is pure code motion.**     |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H6    | expand | The durable-storage seam (§4.2): `dao.host.storage` per host under `src/clj`,  | Green; `file_test` (18), `fs_test` (4), btree durability, journal |\n|       | +migr. | `src/cljs`, `src/cljd`; `dao.jing.file`, `dao.space.store.fs`, `store`,        | and transactor suites unchanged in count and result; kill/restart |\n|       |        | `journal.file`, `btree.storage` portable over a seam value the composition     | and competing-process fixtures (R: named in the storage           |\n|       |        | passes. Largest slice; split per namespace, `jing.file` first.                 | docstrings) still pass; the seam's capability map replaces        |\n|       |        |                                                                                | `directory-sync-supported?`. Allowlist loses five files.          |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H7    | expand | Parser stage as a declared seam value; one test composes the stage on one      | Green; counts unchanged plus the remote-stage test; packet        |\n|       |        | side of an in-memory `dao.stream` and the lowering on the other, proving the   | goldens unchanged.                                                |\n|       |        | stage is relocatable without code change.                                      |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H8    | expand | `dao.stream.conformance-test` over a scripted seam, run against ws, datagram,  | Green; one named suite; each implementation's declared outcome    |\n|       |        | journal and the in-memory implementations; `yin.vm.engine.md` §E.5 gains a     | subset documented and asserted; doc cross-link.                   |\n|       |        | one-paragraph pointer to §4.5.                                                 |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|       |        | **Phase B boundary: slow tier once; H0's allowlist is now `dao/host/*` plus    |                                                                   |\n|       |        | the seam files only.**                                                         |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nDependencies: H1 before H2, H2 before H3 and H4 (both need `bytes`), H5\nindependent after H1; H6 after H2 (bytes) and H3 (addresses), H7 and H8\nindependent after H0. H0-H5 touch none of `dao.stream.ws`, `.transit`,\n`.cbor`, `.cbor.boring`, `.codec`, `remote_channel`, the four ws adapters or\n`yin.repl.serve/connect`; the codec track's C2-C4 edit exactly those, so the\ntwo tracks are file-disjoint and may run concurrently. H2's one touch of\n`dao.stream.codec` (reading `host-bytes?` through `dao.host.bytes`) is\ndeferred until the codec track's C4 has landed, or done by that track in C4\nif it prefers; either way it is one require. The codec track is **not**\nabsorbed: it owns the wire codec; this document owns the host floor beneath\nevery codec.\n\nRisk to the three lanes: low for H1-H5 (code motion behind byte oracles;\nthe known traps are ClojureDart's `:clj` reader feature, so every new file\nnames `:cljd` first, and cljd's protocol this-param cast). Medium for H6: the\nstorage namespaces are the least tested across hosts (R: the spike never\nexercised them) and Node's claim-file lock has process-liveness semantics\nthat must be reproduced exactly behind the seam; split per namespace and\nkeep the existing fixtures as the gate. H4 risk is bounded by the corpus:\neither the bytes match or the slice does not land.\n\n## 9. Cost and what would reverse this\n\nCost, orders of magnitude (P, from the §1 census):\n- Files edited: ~10^1 for phase A (about 20 of 183 portable files, most by\n  a require change and arm deletion; 4-6 new `dao.host.*` files); ~10^1\n  more for phase B (five storage files, three new seam files, two test\n  suites). Nothing in `yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.semantic.*`, `dao.space.index`\n  or `dao.space.query` is touched.\n- Tests: rerun ~10^3 per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000 tests; the\n  `dao.stream` and `dao.jing` subsystems about 690 per lane); rewritten ~10^1\n  (the tests that construct storage or sign keys directly, and\n  `integer_test`'s shim cases, which move with the shim); added ~10^1.\n\nWhat would reverse the design:\n- An owner ruling that reader-conditional arms in portable files are the\n  intended port mechanism (a port is then \"add a feature arm in ~15 files\",\n  which the spike showed is about 150 lines, R). That is cheaper per port\n  and leaves no enumeration, no oracle per entry, and a library requirement\n  per host; this design's value is the enumeration, not fewer lines.\n- A measured regression in H4 (the portable Jing writer) that matters on a\n  hot path. Then the writer stays portable as the oracle and a host may\n  supply a faster one behind the same profile, which is a one-slice change\n  and keeps the byte contract.\n- A finding that the storage seam (§4.2) cannot express a host's lock or\n  durability semantics as data (for example a host with no file locks). Then\n  that host declares the capability absent and the composition refuses\n  durable storage there; the seam shape stands.\n- A decision to make the primitive profile itself stream-shaped. This\n  document argues that is excluded by the hot-path line; if the owner\n  disagrees, §2's table is where the disagreement is recorded.\n\n## 10. Open owner decisions\n\nThese are the owner's, not the Architect's. Recorded here when made.\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| Id   | Question                                                                                             | Architect's recommendation (not a decision)          |\n+======+======================================================================================================+======================================================+\n| OQ1  | Scope: the whole portable core, or only the `yin.repl` closure a new host needs (GUI, graphics and   | The `yin.repl` closure (§3's rows). GUI and graphics |\n|      | demo namespaces excluded)?                                                                           | are compositions with their own seams and no corpus  |\n|      |                                                                                                      | oracle; include them only if a host needs them.      |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ2  | The primitive profile's namespace family name (`dao.host.*` proposed) and whether `dao.data.arrays`  | `dao.host.*`; leave `dao.data.arrays` in place and   |\n|      | moves into it or stays as the btree's own seam.                                                      | list it as a profile member by reference.            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ3  | Whether in-flight host-lane work that adds reader arms to the portable core pauses until phase A     | Pause arm-adding work in the files H1-H3 touch;      |\n|      | lands, or proceeds and is re-based onto `dao.host.*` afterwards.                                     | boundary-agnostic probes (sockets, FFI, parser       |\n|      |                                                                                                      | targets) proceed. Re-basing is one `require` change  |\n|      |                                                                                                      | per file either way.                                 |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ4  | H4: drop Boring and the Dart `cbor` package from the Jing path for a portable writer, or keep them    | Drop them. One writer is the strongest form of \"Jing |\n|      | and only consolidate the shims.                                                                      | is syntax\"; the corpus makes the slice safe.         |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ5  | H6 shape: the seam of §4.2 (recommended), or a redesign of storage as stream implementations all the  | The seam. The journal is already the stream; the     |\n|      | way down (a \"durable cell\" stream for HEAD, a lease-like stream for the lock).                       | all-the-way-down redesign is a separate design with  |\n|      |                                                                                                      | its own document if the owner wants it.              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ6  | Who orchestrates: this track is independent of the codec track and of any host-lane track and should | A separate orchestrator seat; phase A can run        |\n|      | have its own seat.                                                                                   | concurrently with the codec track's C2-C4 (file-     |\n|      |                                                                                                      | disjoint, §8).                                       |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n\n## 11. Decision record\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| Id   | Decision                                                                                                         | Status                       |\n+======+==================================================================================================================+==============================+\n| HD1  | Two kinds of host dependency: world facts cross as `dao.stream` implementations with seam values; machine-        | Architect, 2026-10-20; needs |\n|      | internal carriers and pure functions form one enumerable primitive profile with oracles. Neither adds a contract | non-Claude sign-off          |\n|      | to `dao.stream`.                                                                                                 |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD2  | The hot-path line: per-step, per-value, per-byte, per-address operations are primitives; per-I/O-event            | Architect, 2026-10-20        |\n|      | operations are stream operations.                                                                                |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD3  | Exact integers, floats, bytes, text, arrays, hashing and signatures are seams (c) with vectors; base64 and the    | Architect, 2026-10-20;       |\n|      | Jing CBOR writer are portable (a); storage, sockets and the parser stage are streams (b) over seam values.        | OQ4/OQ5 may amend            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD4  | Clock, entropy and scheduling are the driver's; the core receives them as data or parameters.                     | Architect, 2026-10-20        |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD5  | Migration is expand/migrate/contract in two phases (H0-H5 code motion, H6-H8 seams and suites), file-disjoint    | Architect, 2026-10-20        |\n|      | from the codec track, byte-identical corpora as acceptance, a grep allowlist as the enumeration gate.            |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n## 12. For a successor orchestrator\n\nDecided (HD1-HD5): the two-kind taxonomy, the hot-path line, the per-kind\nanswers, the driver rule, the slice sequence. Not decided: OQ1-OQ6; none of\nthem blocks H0 or H1. OQ2 (the name) should be settled before H1 creates\nfiles; OQ4 before H4; OQ5 before H6; OQ3 before any host-lane work edits the\nH1-H3 files.\n\nStart with **H0**: it changes no source, and the allowlist it records is what\nevery later slice is measured against. Then H1 (new files only, consumers\nuntouched). H2 is the first slice that deletes code; its gate is the corpus\nand the integer fixtures. Run phase A to the slow tier before starting H6.\n\nLanes and gates (`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`, Commands and Gate Ladder):\niterate on the JVM lane with `bb test:sub:clj dao.jing` or `yin.vm` (G0); at\neach checkpoint run the three-lane subsystem test (G1); before landing run\n`bb test:changed` across all three lanes in the worktree (G2); `bb test` on\nmaster after merge is the mandatory gate (G3). Record per-lane test and\nassertion counts before and after every slice. The Dart lane needs `flutter`\non PATH; a fresh worktree needs `npm ci` before the Node lane. ClojureDart\ntraps that bite exactly this kind of file: `:cljd` must be the **first**\nfeature in every mixed conditional (the host-eval pass also matches `:clj`);\na tail-position `:cljd nil` silently fails; protocol method parameters must\nnot be `[_ _]`; `(- 0.0)` is `+0.0`; multi-key `assoc` on nil misbehaves.\n\nReview requirement: this design and any implementation an orchestrator\nderives from it under a Claude model are Claude-authored. Sign-off on the\ndesign and on each slice must come from a non-Claude architect; a Claude\nreviewer may review but may not be the sole sign-off. The reviewer checks, in\norder: that no slice deletes an arm a later slice still needs (expand before\ncontract); that every H1-H4 acceptance is byte-identical against the named\ncorpus on all three lanes; that the allowlist shrinks monotonically and the\nnegative test still fails; that H6 preserves the Node claim-file liveness\nrule and the HEAD rename atomicity exactly; that nothing in this track edits\na codec-track file while that track's C2-C4 are in flight.\n\n## Appendix A. Citations opened for this document (2026-10-20, read only)\n\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Cite                                                         | Finding                                                                                          |\n+==============================================================+==================================================================================================+\n| `docs/design/datom.world.md:16-32,43-48,68-71`               | Axioms; streams; \"A host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary\".                       |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/datom.world.abi.md:21-24,35-44,47-52,56-64,`    | One ABI, many implementations; custody; machine-internal work inside the instance; the floor is  |\n| `83-87,94-148`                                               | world facts; effects out, events in; adapter discipline; \"two things are not adapters\".          |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:14-27,29-52,96-105`          | Import surface is one implementation; in-instance ring buffer needs no crossing; Jing bytes.     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:707-894`                       | E.5 conformance method; E.5.2 codec/identity gates; E.5.3 W0 adapter probe list; E.5.4 risks.    |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.codec.md:1-50,140-180,616-637,`      | Status; contract namespace; a host without Transit; slice sequence and file set; wire proof.     |\n| `658-750,782-883`                                            |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc:1-130` (299 lines)        | Carrier table; \"depends on nothing\"; 30 arms; promote/demote.                                    |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:1-60` (913 lines)              | Kernels written once over the shim; native fast path; algorithms portable.                       |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:1-160`                               | Hash libraries per host; content-store handle is plain data.                                     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1-60,95-254,525-560,1140,1553`  | Decode is Jing's own reader; Boring/Dart writer selection; private big-integer helpers; float    |\n|                                                              | bits; `encode`/`decode` entry points.                                                            |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc:1-60`;                   | Wire-tree vocabulary; `:cljd nil` gating; Dart twin 107 lines.                                   |\n| `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd` (wc)                      |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:1-40,163-259`                   | Framing and recovery portable; random-access host calls; \"not a stream (Decision 2)\".            |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc:1-60,77-82,198-210,`       | Lock semantics per host incl. Node claim files; temp+fsync+rename; directory sync capability.   |\n| `265,292-314,352-378,419-448`                                |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/stream/journal/file.cljc:1-70`;                | The journal is a dao.stream over the content file under the directory lock; durability map.      |\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.journal.md:1-25`                     |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:1-60,138-148`                 | Host arrays; `^long` hint discipline; `parallelSort`.                                            |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc:1-70`                      | Ed25519 libraries; hex/domain/message assembly portable; `SecureRandom`.                         |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:70-180`                           | `integral`, code units, `units->string` arms.                                                    |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc:1-70`;                    | Seam contract (deposit writer, `{:send! :close!}`); JVM receiver thread inside the seam.         |\n| `src/clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj:1-50`                   |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc`; `src/clj/yin/repl/host/`      | `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}` seam value; per-host shadows.                                      |\n| `jvm.clj`                                                    |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:621-652`                        | `accept-connection!` takes `now`; seam carries `:ws/subprotocol`.                                |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj:1-40`                 | Sleep machinery is host policy; holds no interpreter state.                                      |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/jing/content/async.cljc:1-60`                  | A timer per host inside a portable file.                                                         |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:137-151`                        | Entropy per host; datagram seam selected by reader arm.                                          |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc:1-76`;               | Parser is a stage between streams; grammar profile is the per-host surface; no ANTLR object      |\n| `src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj:1-40`; `src/cljc/yin/repl/`      | leaves; the catalog names the Python namespaces.                                                 |\n| `frontends.cljc:1-50`                                        |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `rg -c '#\\?' src/cljc`; `rg -n '#\\?'` over `yin/vm.cljc`,    | The census of §1; `catch` arms dominate the VM, linker and REPL counts.                          |\n| `engine`, `linker`, `repl`, `debruijn`, `data`, `values`,    |                                                                                                  |\n| `dao/stream/*`, `dao/space/*`, `dao/jing/dht`                |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `wc -l` over the per-host seam files and                     | Sizes quoted in §1 and §5.                                                                       |\n| `src/cljc/{dao,yin}` host-dependency files                   |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc`, `integer_v3_fixtures.cljc`, | Deftest counts (26, 23, 40, 18, 4, 5, 6) and the oracles named in §4.3.                          |\n| `test/dao/jing/{cbor_test,cbor_fixtures,cbor_conformance_    |                                                                                                  |\n| test,file_test,hash_registry_contract_test}.cljc`,           |                                                                                                  |\n| `test/dao/jing_test.cljc`, `test/dao/space/store/fs_test.    |                                                                                                  |\n| cljc`, `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_test.cljc`                   |                                                                                                  |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `git log` (`8351e639`, `eacaed90`)                           | Codec track C0 and C1 landed on master.                                                          |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/orchestrator-log.md:11739,12375`                       | R: JVM quick tier about 4,094 tests; `dao.stream`+`dao.jing` subsystem 689/680/657 per lane.     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `collab/1792412960461-engineer-jolt-yin-repl-spike.claude-   | R: 86 of 91 namespaces loaded unchanged on a fourth host; failures at the §1 rows; ~150 overlay  |\n| opus-5-5.findings.md`                                        | lines; storage namespaces not exercised.                                                         |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
