{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '470,710p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; cat docs/design/datom.world.abi.md; cat docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md\"","aggregated_output":"   mutation on rejected adapter calls\") are exactly this suite instantiated\n   for the wasm implementation. The design makes W0's list the shared list\n   and runs it against the socket and journal seams too.\n\nThe wasm import surface is unaffected: it is one implementation of §4.1 and\nits canonical bytes are Jing's (`dao.stream.wasm.md` §3). A portable Jing\nwriter (§3) makes the oracle for those bytes one implementation rather than\nthree, which strengthens E.5.2 without changing it.\n\n## 5. What porting a host consists of, after this design\n\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| #   | Step                                                                  | Size and proof                                                             |\n+=====+=======================================================================+============================================================================+\n| 1   | Choose carriers and implement `dao.host.*` (§4.3) for the dialect.    | ~55 functions; green `dao.host.conformance-test`. Hash and signature        |\n|     |                                                                       | entries bind a library (owner guidance); everything else is the dialect's  |\n|     |                                                                       | own arithmetic, strings and arrays.                                        |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 2   | Load the portable core with a reader feature for the dialect only     | Zero source edits in `src/cljc` outside `dao.host.*`: the grep gate (§8    |\n|     | where the dialect's syntax requires it.                               | H0) is what proves this.                                                   |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 3   | Run the pure-evaluator milestone: in-memory streams, local REPL.       | The existing `yin.vm` and `yin.repl` suites minus network and storage;    |\n|     |                                                                       | §E.5 engine fixtures.                                                      |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 4   | Implement the seam values the composition needs, one per milestone:   | Each ~100-350 lines (V: today's seams); green `dao.stream.conformance-test`|\n|     | durable storage (§4.2), datagram, WebSocket, parser stage.            | per implementation, then the existing cross-host peer suites.              |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| 5   | Write `main` (§4.4).                                                  | Host-specific by nature; the REPL shell tests.                             |\n+-----+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nFor a native Clojure dialect on a Scheme runtime (the example a recent spike\nexercised, R), steps 1 and 2 are the whole difference from today's spike\noverlays: the four overlay files become one `dao.host.*` implementation with\nvectors, and the content writer needs no port because it is portable. For a\nRust peer, step 1 is the Rust value model (already planned in\n`yin.vm.rust.md`) and step 4 is its native `dao.stream` peer; this document\nadds the conformance suites it would run.\n\n## 6. Verifiability\n\n- **Byte-level oracles exist for the hot primitives**: the cbor-v1 corpus,\n  the integer v3 fixtures, the sign vectors, the hash registry test. The\n  design moves code behind them without changing a byte: every slice in §8\n  is acceptance-tested as \"identical corpus bytes and identical fixture\n  results on three lanes\".\n- **Enumerability is mechanical**: after H0 a grep over `src/cljc` for host\n  arms outside `dao/host/` and the named seam files is empty, and CI keeps it\n  empty.\n- **Seams are testable without hosts**: a scripted seam value (a map of\n  recording functions) drives the stream-implementation suite on every lane\n  without a socket or a disk, which is how E.5.1 already tests adapters\n  (\"scripted transports exist in every composition\").\n\n## 7. Migration principles\n\nExpand, migrate, contract (the codec track's sequence, `codec.md` §7.1, V):\nadd the new namespace, move consumers one at a time, delete the old arms.\nEach slice lands alone under the quick tier on three lanes with recorded\ntest and assertion counts; \"unchanged\" means equal counts plus any added\ntest named. Byte-identical acceptance wherever a corpus exists. The slow\ntier runs once at the end of phase A and once at the end of phase B (owner\ncadence: slow tier at phase boundaries).\n\n## 8. Slices, smallest first\n\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Phase  | Change                                                                         | Falsifiable acceptance                                            |\n+=======+========+================================================================================+===================================================================+\n| H0    | freeze | No `src/` change. A committed allowlist of files permitted to contain host     | The gate passes on the current tree with exactly today's list;   |\n|       |        | arms (today's §1 table) and a test that fails when any other `src/cljc` file   | it fails when a `#?(:clj` is added to a file outside the list     |\n|       |        | gains one. Record per-lane counts. Record the Jing corpus hash set.            | (one negative test). Counts recorded.                             |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H1    | expand | Create `dao.host.integer`, `dao.host.float`, `dao.host.bytes`, `dao.host.text` | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus `dao.host.conformance-   |\n|       |        | with today's arms moved verbatim, plus `dao.host.conformance-test` running the | test` named; cbor corpus bytes identical; `integer_v3_fixtures`   |\n|       |        | existing oracles against them. No consumer changes.                            | unchanged. Allowlist grows by the four files.                     |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H2    | migr.  | `yin.vm.integer` and `dao.jing.cbor` require `dao.host.integer`/`float`;       | Green; counts unchanged; corpus identical; `grep BigInteger       |\n|       |        | `cbor.cljc:106-254` and `yin.vm.integer.host` deleted. `chunks`, `jing.dht`,   | src/cljc` hits only `dao/host/integer.cljc`; allowlist shrinks by |\n|       |        | `linker`, `sign`, `datagram`, `codec`, `data`, `debruijn` read `bytes`/`text`. | the migrated files.                                               |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H3    | expand | `dao.host.hash` and `dao.host.sign`; `dao.jing` and `yin.vm.linker.sign`       | Green; counts unchanged plus the vector tests named; every Jing   |\n|       | +migr. | require them; their arms deleted. Base64 made portable over `bytes`.           | address in the corpus identical; `sign_vectors.edn` passes;       |\n|       |        |                                                                                | `grep MessageDigest\\|Blake3\\|noble\\|ed25519 src/cljc` hits only   |\n|       |        |                                                                                | `dao/host/`.                                                      |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H4    | migr.  | Portable Jing CBOR writer over `dao.host.*`; `dao.jing.cbor` requires it on    | Green; `cbor-v1.json`, `cbor_fixtures`, `cbor_conformance_test`   |\n|       |        | every host; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` and `dao.jing.cbor.cljd` deleted; the       | byte-identical on three lanes; the frozen DHT and linker image    |\n|       |        | Boring dependency stays only for `dao.stream.cbor.boring` (codec track's).     | goldens unchanged; a write-throughput measurement recorded        |\n|       |        |                                                                                | against Boring on the JVM (informational; not a gate).            |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H5    | migr.  | Entropy and scheduler to composition parameters: `dao.space.dht` and           | Green; counts unchanged; `grep SecureRandom\\|getRandomValues\\|    |\n|       |        | `yin.vm.linker.sign` take `random-bytes`; `dao.jing.content.async` takes       | setTimeout src/cljc` hits only `yin/repl/main.cljc` (the driver)  |\n|       |        | `schedule`; `yin.repl.main` supplies them.                                     | or nothing.                                                       |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|       |        | **Phase A boundary: slow tier once. Everything above is pure code motion.**     |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H6    | expand | The durable-storage seam (§4.2): `dao.host.storage` per host under `src/clj`,  | Green; `file_test` (18), `fs_test` (4), btree durability, journal |\n|       | +migr. | `src/cljs`, `src/cljd`; `dao.jing.file`, `dao.space.store.fs`, `store`,        | and transactor suites unchanged in count and result; kill/restart |\n|       |        | `journal.file`, `btree.storage` portable over a seam value the composition     | and competing-process fixtures (R: named in the storage           |\n|       |        | passes. Largest slice; split per namespace, `jing.file` first.                 | docstrings) still pass; the seam's capability map replaces        |\n|       |        |                                                                                | `directory-sync-supported?`. Allowlist loses five files.          |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H7    | expand | Parser stage as a declared seam value; one test composes the stage on one      | Green; counts unchanged plus the remote-stage test; packet        |\n|       |        | side of an in-memory `dao.stream` and the lowering on the other, proving the   | goldens unchanged.                                                |\n|       |        | stage is relocatable without code change.                                      |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H8    | expand | `dao.stream.conformance-test` over a scripted seam, run against ws, datagram,  | Green; one named suite; each implementation's declared outcome    |\n|       |        | journal and the in-memory implementations; `yin.vm.engine.md` §E.5 gains a     | subset documented and asserted; doc cross-link.                   |\n|       |        | one-paragraph pointer to §4.5.                                                 |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|       |        | **Phase B boundary: slow tier once; H0's allowlist is now `dao/host/*` plus    |                                                                   |\n|       |        | the seam files only.**                                                         |                                                                   |\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nDependencies: H1 before H2, H2 before H3 and H4 (both need `bytes`), H5\nindependent after H1; H6 after H2 (bytes) and H3 (addresses), H7 and H8\nindependent after H0. H0-H5 touch none of `dao.stream.ws`, `.transit`,\n`.cbor`, `.cbor.boring`, `.codec`, `remote_channel`, the four ws adapters or\n`yin.repl.serve/connect`; the codec track's C2-C4 edit exactly those, so the\ntwo tracks are file-disjoint and may run concurrently. H2's one touch of\n`dao.stream.codec` (reading `host-bytes?` through `dao.host.bytes`) is\ndeferred until the codec track's C4 has landed, or done by that track in C4\nif it prefers; either way it is one require. The codec track is **not**\nabsorbed: it owns the wire codec; this document owns the host floor beneath\nevery codec.\n\nRisk to the three lanes: low for H1-H5 (code motion behind byte oracles;\nthe known traps are ClojureDart's `:clj` reader feature, so every new file\nnames `:cljd` first, and cljd's protocol this-param cast). Medium for H6: the\nstorage namespaces are the least tested across hosts (R: the spike never\nexercised them) and Node's claim-file lock has process-liveness semantics\nthat must be reproduced exactly behind the seam; split per namespace and\nkeep the existing fixtures as the gate. H4 risk is bounded by the corpus:\neither the bytes match or the slice does not land.\n\n## 9. Cost and what would reverse this\n\nCost, orders of magnitude (P, from the §1 census):\n- Files edited: ~10^1 for phase A (about 20 of 183 portable files, most by\n  a require change and arm deletion; 4-6 new `dao.host.*` files); ~10^1\n  more for phase B (five storage files, three new seam files, two test\n  suites). Nothing in `yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.semantic.*`, `dao.space.index`\n  or `dao.space.query` is touched.\n- Tests: rerun ~10^3 per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000 tests; the\n  `dao.stream` and `dao.jing` subsystems about 690 per lane); rewritten ~10^1\n  (the tests that construct storage or sign keys directly, and\n  `integer_test`'s shim cases, which move with the shim); added ~10^1.\n\nWhat would reverse the design:\n- An owner ruling that reader-conditional arms in portable files are the\n  intended port mechanism (a port is then \"add a feature arm in ~15 files\",\n  which the spike showed is about 150 lines, R). That is cheaper per port\n  and leaves no enumeration, no oracle per entry, and a library requirement\n  per host; this design's value is the enumeration, not fewer lines.\n- A measured regression in H4 (the portable Jing writer) that matters on a\n  hot path. Then the writer stays portable as the oracle and a host may\n  supply a faster one behind the same profile, which is a one-slice change\n  and keeps the byte contract.\n- A finding that the storage seam (§4.2) cannot express a host's lock or\n  durability semantics as data (for example a host with no file locks). Then\n  that host declares the capability absent and the composition refuses\n  durable storage there; the seam shape stands.\n- A decision to make the primitive profile itself stream-shaped. This\n  document argues that is excluded by the hot-path line; if the owner\n  disagrees, §2's table is where the disagreement is recorded.\n\n## 10. Open owner decisions\n\nThese are the owner's, not the Architect's. Recorded here when made.\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| Id   | Question                                                                                             | Architect's recommendation (not a decision)          |\n+======+======================================================================================================+======================================================+\n| OQ1  | Scope: the whole portable core, or only the `yin.repl` closure a new host needs (GUI, graphics and   | The `yin.repl` closure (§3's rows). GUI and graphics |\n|      | demo namespaces excluded)?                                                                           | are compositions with their own seams and no corpus  |\n|      |                                                                                                      | oracle; include them only if a host needs them.      |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ2  | The primitive profile's namespace family name (`dao.host.*` proposed) and whether `dao.data.arrays`  | `dao.host.*`; leave `dao.data.arrays` in place and   |\n|      | moves into it or stays as the btree's own seam.                                                      | list it as a profile member by reference.            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ3  | Whether in-flight host-lane work that adds reader arms to the portable core pauses until phase A     | Pause arm-adding work in the files H1-H3 touch;      |\n|      | lands, or proceeds and is re-based onto `dao.host.*` afterwards.                                     | boundary-agnostic probes (sockets, FFI, parser       |\n|      |                                                                                                      | targets) proceed. Re-basing is one `require` change  |\n|      |                                                                                                      | per file either way.                                 |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ4  | H4: drop Boring and the Dart `cbor` package from the Jing path for a portable writer, or keep them    | Drop them. One writer is the strongest form of \"Jing |\n|      | and only consolidate the shims.                                                                      | is syntax\"; the corpus makes the slice safe.         |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ5  | H6 shape: the seam of §4.2 (recommended), or a redesign of storage as stream implementations all the  | The seam. The journal is already the stream; the     |\n|      | way down (a \"durable cell\" stream for HEAD, a lease-like stream for the lock).                       | all-the-way-down redesign is a separate design with  |\n|      |                                                                                                      | its own document if the owner wants it.              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ6  | Who orchestrates: this track is independent of the codec track and of any host-lane track and should | A separate orchestrator seat; phase A can run        |\n|      | have its own seat.                                                                                   | concurrently with the codec track's C2-C4 (file-     |\n|      |                                                                                                      | disjoint, §8).                                       |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------+\n\n## 11. Decision record\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| Id   | Decision                                                                                                         | Status                       |\n+======+==================================================================================================================+==============================+\n| HD1  | Two kinds of host dependency: world facts cross as `dao.stream` implementations with seam values; machine-        | Architect, 2026-10-20; needs |\n|      | internal carriers and pure functions form one enumerable primitive profile with oracles. Neither adds a contract | non-Claude sign-off          |\n|      | to `dao.stream`.                                                                                                 |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD2  | The hot-path line: per-step, per-value, per-byte, per-address operations are primitives; per-I/O-event            | Architect, 2026-10-20        |\n|      | operations are stream operations.                                                                                |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD3  | Exact integers, floats, bytes, text, arrays, hashing and signatures are seams (c) with vectors; base64 and the    | Architect, 2026-10-20;       |\n|      | Jing CBOR writer are portable (a); storage, sockets and the parser stage are streams (b) over seam values.        | OQ4/OQ5 may amend            |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD4  | Clock, entropy and scheduling are the driver's; the core receives them as data or parameters.                     | Architect, 2026-10-20        |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n| HD5  | Migration is expand/migrate/contract in two phases (H0-H5 code motion, H6-H8 seams and suites), file-disjoint    | Architect, 2026-10-20        |\n|      | from the codec track, byte-identical corpora as acceptance, a grep allowlist as the enumeration gate.            |                              |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n## 12. For a successor orchestrator\n\nDecided (HD1-HD5): the two-kind taxonomy, the hot-path line, the per-kind\nanswers, the driver rule, the slice sequence. Not decided: OQ1-OQ6; none of\nthem blocks H0 or H1. OQ2 (the name) should be settled before H1 creates\nfiles; OQ4 before H4; OQ5 before H6; OQ3 before any host-lane work edits the\nH1-H3 files.\n\nStart with **H0**: it changes no source, and the allowlist it records is what\nevery later slice is measured against. Then H1 (new files only, consumers\nuntouched). H2 is the first slice that deletes code; its gate is the corpus\nand the integer fixtures. Run phase A to the slow tier before starting H6.\n\nLanes and gates (`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`, Commands and Gate Ladder):\niterate on the JVM lane with `bb test:sub:clj dao.jing` or `yin.vm` (G0); at\neach checkpoint run the three-lane subsystem test (G1); before landing run\n`bb test:changed` across all three lanes in the worktree (G2); `bb test` on\nmaster after merge is the mandatory gate (G3). Record per-lane test and\nassertion counts before and after every slice. The Dart lane needs `flutter`\non PATH; a fresh worktree needs `npm ci` before the Node lane. ClojureDart\ntraps that bite exactly this kind of file: `:cljd` must be the **first**\nfeature in every mixed conditional (the host-eval pass also matches `:clj`);\na tail-position `:cljd nil` silently fails; protocol method parameters must\n# datom.world ABI: the one abstraction boundary\n\nStatus: design/doctrine; owner rulings, 2026-10-19.\n\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md) owns **what** the contract is: operations,\nexhaustive outcome maps, canonical values, batch and observer semantics. This\ndocument owns **why** that contract is the only boundary: role, rulings,\nimplementation taxonomy, floor and custody. It does not redefine the operations.\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): distilled from this session's owner rulings and moved\nfrom datom.world.md, Host Boundaries. The five-round wasm consensus lineage\nis recorded in [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md); subsequent owner rulings below\nsupersede its former service-protocol framing. This is the single doctrine home.\n\nAuthorities:\n- [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md): master axioms and commitments.\n- [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the abstraction contract.\n- [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md): execution, custody and conformance method.\n- [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md): concrete machine state and fixtures.\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n## 1. The system ABI\n\nOne abstraction boundary (`dao.stream`), many\nimplementations (ring-buffer, websocket, UDP, wasm imports). `dao.stream` is\nthe contract for datom.world and `yin.vm`: operations, exhaustive outcome maps,\ncanonical values, append-only batch semantics and observer discipline. It is\nnot a transport implementation or a wire protocol. The wasm import-surface\nimplementation is specified once in [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md);\nits canonical Jing CBOR representation does not define the abstraction itself.\n\n## 2. Implementations and the taxonomy ruling\n\n**Owner clarification, 2026-10-19 (verbatim):** “dao.stream is an abstraction\nboundary. It is not a transport implementation. It can be implemented using a\nring-buffer, a websocket or UDP”\n\nThe wasm import surface is another implementation: adapters implement the\nabstraction through imported functions over linear memory. A co-resident\nproducer can instead use an in-memory ring-buffer implementation inside the\ninstance with zero host crossings. Both preserve the same abstraction contract.\n\n## 3. Custody at the boundary\n\nWhat crosses a trust domain is a strict subset of in-machine values: closures\nand continuations cross only as admissible portable plain data; host types,\nresources and code artifacts never do. The custody gates retain this distinction\n([yin.vm.linker.md](./yin.vm.linker.md)); an implementation grants no additional\nportability or authority.\n\n## 4. Boundary ruling and supersession\n\n**Owner ruling, strengthened 2026-10-19: the boundary is drawn, not found;\none abstraction boundary is the principle.** The boundary between wasm and datom.world is\n`dao.stream`; its implementation uses whatever wasm exposes to the outside\nworld. The exposed functions are **adapters to dao.stream**, which is the ABI\nto `yin.vm` and the whole ecosystem. Machine-internal work executes inside the\nwasm instance: the Rust engine crates and compiled register bodies share memory\nand direct calls. Only effects/facts cross through the stream adapters. World\nfacts still require interaction with the world; moving engine implementation\ninside the machine does not manufacture those facts.\n\n**Dated supersession (owner boundary clarification, 2026-10-19):** This replaces\nthe earlier same-day “sole justification … performance” allowance for\nprocess-local services and the service-protocol interpretation of\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` (historically renamed from `yin.register.host.v1`). Its private operation vocabulary, TLV codec,\nexpanded-canonical-bytes comparator and service-status protocol are dissolved.\nPerformance work optimizes internal execution or the stream implementation; it does\nnot reintroduce a semantic service interface. [yang.wasm.md](./yang.wasm.md)\nis the compiler implementation plan for this in-instance architecture.\n\n## 5. Naming and the floor\n\n“The system ABI” names only `dao.stream`. Earlier qualified guest-host service\nnames are historical provenance for the dissolved protocol, not names for a\nsecond contract. Implementation specs name their implementation (WebSocket,\nwasm import surface), never “the wasm ABI”. Internal compiler linkage does not\ncreate an ecosystem boundary.\n\nThe floor is the need to obtain world facts that shipped code cannot compute.\nThat seam is immovable; the engineering frontier is movable: machine-internal\noperations can move inside the instance without changing the abstraction.\nAn in-instance stream between co-resident producers and observers needs no host\ncrossing. Changing physical placement does not remove external facts or custody.\n\nImplementations include ring-buffer, [WebSocket](./dao.stream.ws.md),\n[wasm imports](./dao.stream.wasm.md), and UDP. These implement one abstraction;\nnone adds an ABI. The import-surface representation and buffer rules belong\nonly to its implementation spec.\n\n## 6. Effects out and events in\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n## 7. Adapter discipline\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n# DaoStream Wasm: the wasm implementation of the dao.stream abstraction\n\nStatus: design, owner-authorized planning; not implemented. Subordinate to\n[dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): that document is the contract; this one\nspecifies the wasm import-surface implementation. Where they disagree, the\ncontract wins. It is a sibling implementation spec to\n[dao.stream.ws.md](./dao.stream.ws.md).\n\nProvenance (2026-10-19): moved without changing the adapter contract from\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 by owner-approved decomposition.\nDoctrine and the dated supersessions have one home,\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md). The prior service-protocol tables\nremain dissolved; this move does not revive them.\n\nThe import surface exists because wasm reaches outside through imported\nfunctions over linear memory. These adapters implement dao.stream operations.\nThe specification is language-neutral: CLJS hosts and native embedders implement\nthe same surface. The Rust host-side implementation plan lives in\n[yin.vm.rust.md](./yin.vm.rust.md) §6.4; the guest engine is not\nimplemented by those host adapters.\n\nWith co-resident producers, an in-instance ring-buffer implementation may\nservice stream operations with zero host crossings. The signatures and Jing\nbytes below specify the import-surface implementation, not a requirement that\nevery implementation serialize or cross into the host. Canonical encoding is\nowned by [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md); engine codec conformance is specified in\n[yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2 and §E.5.\n\n## 1. Imported adapter surface\n\nUse import module `dao.stream` with the following proposed import names.\nAll parameters and single results are i32. `p,n` address one canonical Jing\nCBOR value; `out,cap` denote a disjoint writable response region. The returned\ni32 is the **encoded response byte length**, never a status number. Decode\nthat response into the operation's exhaustive outcome map. Zero-copy views\nmust not outlive the synchronous call or memory growth.\n\n| Import | Parameters → result | Existing stream operation |\n|---|---|---|\n| `create` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `create!` on a creation specification (VM make-stream adapter). |\n| `attach` | `(p,n,out,cap) → length` | `attach!` on a portable descriptor. |\n| `descriptor` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `descriptor`. |\n| `cursor` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `cursor` with decoded anchor. |\n| `next` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `next` with decoded cursor; the poll operation. |\n| `append` | `(handle,p,n,out,cap) → length` | `append!` with decoded value. |\n| `close` | `(handle,out,cap) → length` | `close!`. |\n\nMake/poll are VM-facing terminology, not additional stream operations. Park,\nresume, resolve-var, primitive application, state reads, roots, collection and\nmodule handling have **no imports**: they execute in-instance. Imports never\nname arbitrary FFI operations. Memory/table provision and module instantiation\nare runtime linkage/lifecycle mechanics, not semantic service operations.\n\nOperational stream handles cannot themselves be canonical data. The import-surface implementation\nrepresents the handle field in a successful create/attach outcome by a checked,\ntask-local i32 slot, encoded as a Jing integer; decoding reconstructs the local\nhandle resource before the engine consumes the outcome. This is the physical\nrepresentation of an existing handle, not a guest stream identity or a portable\ncapability. Keep this adapter handle namespace distinct from ValueRefs, check\nownership/lifetime, and never publish it in a stream value, continuation or\nmodule payload. Portable descriptors/cursors and all other outcome fields use\ntheir actual canonical data. Repeated reads remain non-destructive; the adapter\nhas no privileged reader and introduces no callbacks.\n\n## 2. Outcome and buffer discipline\n\nThe exhaustive outcome sets and required keys are those of [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md),\nCreating and Attaching, Cursors, Reading, Writing and Closing. Preserve optional\nqualified diagnostics and retry declarations. Preserve the stream contract’s\nunknown-outcome rule: not ok, nothing observed, write acceptance unknown, no\nautomatic retry without its declared retry permission. In particular append returns\nok/full/invalid-value/closed/refused/transport-error; next returns\nok/blocked/end/gap/cursor-mismatch/invalid-cursor/refused/transport-error;\nclose has only ok. Do not turn ordinary blocking or refusal into a trap.\n\nThe engine allocates request/response memory before invoking an adapter. The\ncomposition declares supported payload/output bounds; W0 establishes a response\nreservation sufficient for **every** admitted result, including diagnostic and\nhandle outcomes. Validate pointers, lengths, overflow, overlap and reservation\nbefore executing an operation. Invalid adapter calls are implementation faults,\nnot new stream statuses. Allocation failure is a qualified local resource limit\nbefore invocation. No operation may commit and then ask the guest to resize and\nfetch its response; no retained-response service or semantic replay exists.\n\nA full append buffer uses the stream's **full** outcome: nothing appended, keep\nthe exact pending value and retry through the existing writer wait discipline.\nResponse capacity is reserved separately; full is not invented for reads or\nclose. Read-side inability to carry an oversized value follows the stream's\ntransport-error outcome without advancing the immutable cursor. Admitted limits\nmust make ordinary outcomes deliverable; test limit failures before mutation.\nAn append transport-error can mean acceptance is unknown: do not automatically\nretry unless that transport declares clean failure. Blocking waits stay as pure\ndata in the in-instance engine; the composition later drives execution again,\nwithout retaining a wasm activation or application callback.\n\n## 3. Canonical bytes and authority\n\nIn the import-surface implementation, requests and outcome data use exactly Jing's canonical CBOR ([yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.2.2), with its\nown ordering, numeric carriers, validation and decode/re-encode rules. Length\nparameters delimit one CBOR value; no TLV or alternate framing wraps it.\nCanonical maps/sets use repository ordering, never task handles or arena ids.\nRegister image checksums still use the register codec. Buffer access grants no\npermission to pass internal Values, private engine snapshots or trusted wrappers\nacross custody gates. Stream history records effects/facts, not register loads,\nroot materialization or other machine-internal traffic.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
