{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1240,1285p' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md; sed -n '98,180p' src/cljc/dao/space/store/fs.cljc; sed -n '163,260p' src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc; rg -n 'stable|asort|overlap|NFC|normalize|blake3|sha256' test/dao/data/arrays_test.cljc test/dao/data/btree_test.cljc test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc; sed -n '1,100p' test/yin/vm/linker/sign_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"+---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice                                       | Boundary          | Falsifiable acceptance                                                                              |\n+=============================================+===================+=====================================================================================================+\n| External dependency: the codec track        | Not this track    | [dao.stream.codec.md](./dao.stream.codec.md) must have landed its slices C0-C4 on master before J6  |\n|                                             |                   | is commissioned; jolt requires nothing else of it (§9.4). Not jolt work; not restated here.          |\n+---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| J0a: tracked load and toolchain             | Manifest and      | Precondition: the owner's disposition of the §13.2 defaults that J0a would apply is recorded.       |\n|                                             | bootstrap, branch | Clean-cache core require without overlays; standard/main dependency audit, naming                   |\n|                                             | inventory, narrow | runtime-unexercised dependencies; after C0-C4, proving `cognitect.transit`/transit-clj/transit-java  |\n|                                             | source            | absent from the jolt closure (ruling 1); pin `96d7b866` recorded with \"tag: none; nearest v0.8.23\"   |\n|                                             |                   | (ruling 4); S arithmetic and six hashes                                                             |\n|                                             |                   | reproduced; gaps listed. Commit the selected Chez selector and an exact CMake pin in `mise.toml`.   |\n|                                             | adaptations,      | Vendored source hashes and build paths; guarded `src/clj` loading; source/provides/binary           |\n|                                             | formatter/linter  | precedence; `.jolt` formatting and kondo; runner; FFI smoke. libcrypto provenance and negative      |\n|                                             | and loader gates  | tests; long-hash collect-safety. Platforms reported independently; both needed for platform         |\n|                                             |                   | qualification.                                                                                      |\n+---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| J0b: UDP FFI feasibility                    | Small raw         | §8.3 on both platforms: packet boundaries, N=1200 and configured-N truncation sentinel, EMSGSIZE,   |\n|                                             | transport on      | landed transport-error and send-failed on EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK and macOS ENOBUFS, nonblocking         |\n|                                             | existing          | receive, cleanup. Injected versus natural results labelled. No `full`, no send wait inside          |\n|                                             | socket/poller     | `append!`.                                                                                          |\n|                                             | groundwork        |                                                                                                     |\n+---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| J0c: early C++ feasibility                  | Python Cpp target | Starts at the §7.8 minimum baseline. §7.8 on both platforms: packet and completeness parity,        |\n|                                             | and helpers,      | restricted lowering corpus, two-worker/cache isolation, collect-safe calls, thread/stack budget,    |\n|                                             | raw-tree wrapper, | exception/lifetime tests, helper provenance, generated digests. No C++ Jing encoder. Failure        |\n|                                             | jolt cst adapter  | returns to the owner; no fallback.                                                                  |\n|                                             | over the shared   |                                                                                                     |\n|                                             | exporter, native  |                                                                                                     |\n|                                             | worker isolation  |                                                                                                     |\n+---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| J1: numeric and content correctness         | Exact integer     | Existing numeric/CBOR/hash corpus bytes and refusals match JVM/CLJS/CLJD; official hash boundaries; |\n|                                             | algorithms, full  | every scalar class and host-value wire node (byte arrays anywhere); decode seams                    |\n|                                             | Jing              | `cbor.cljc:537,649-652`; >64-bit integers and `~x` identities; correct code-index sort. No SHA      |\n|                                             | representation    | stub, no native-float wire shortcut.                                                                |\n|                                             | and wire, C       |                                                                                                     |\n|                                             | BLAKE3, libcrypto |                                                                                                     |\n|                                             | SHA, `asort`      |                                                                                                     |\n+---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| J2: pure test lane and VM conformance       | `test:jolt`       | Every applicable pure test loads and passes; semantic and register boundary traces match. No silent |\n|                                             | family, shared    | excludes, unchanged goldens, expected counts, nonzero failure exit. A throwing test is an error and |\n|                                             | fixture adapter,  | later tests still run: reproduce `docs/jolt-experiment.md:70` with sentinel tests after it and a    |\n|                                             | B-tree/space/VM   | nonzero final status. Shared source changes keep the existing three lanes green. On jolt:           |\n|                                             | pure suites,      | `dao.stream.transit` is not loaded; codec-less `make-attacher`/`make-endpoint` throw at             |\n|                                             | runtime bug fixes | composition; Transit-codec shared tests excluded with reason; codec-neutral suites run over the      |\n|                                             |                   | CBOR profile (§9.4).                                                                                |\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- claim-entries\n     \"Every Node claim entry in the directory, as `[file-name pid]`: the\n      files `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, each created by the one process whose\n      pid it names.\"\n     [fs dir]\n     (keep (fn [file-name]\n             (when-some [[_ pid] (re-matches #\"lock\\.(\\d+)\\.[0-9a-f-]+\"\n                                             file-name)]\n               [file-name (js/parseInt pid 10)]))\n           (.readdirSync ^js fs dir))))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn- node-lock!\n     \"Claim the directory on Node, answering the release, or refuse.\n\n      Each contender creates its own uniquely named claim entry,\n      `lock.<pid>.<nonce>`, and only then reads the others.  An entry\n      naming a live process is an owner or a rival contender: the claim\n      is withdrawn and refused.  An entry naming a dead process — an\n      owner that crashed, or a contender that crashed midway through its\n      own claim — is removed, and that removal is race-free because the\n      name is unique: no later process ever creates it again, so it can\n      never be a newer live claim.  Of two contenders, each creates\n      before it reads, so at most one reads no live rival; at worst two\n      simultaneous starts both refuse, never both own.\n\n      An entry naming this very pid is live like any other: worker\n      threads share the process's pid but each has its own `held`, so\n      another worker's claim looks exactly like that — only this call's\n      own entry, by its exact name, is this contender's.  Liveness is by\n      pid, so a dead claim whose pid was reused by an unrelated live\n      process is read as live and refuses; the refusal names the claim\n      entries it saw, for the operator remedy in the namespace\n      docstring.\"\n     [dir claimed]\n     (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")\n           own-pid (.-pid js/process)\n           own (str lock-name \".\" own-pid \".\" (random-uuid))\n           own-path (str dir \"/\" own)\n           release! (fn []\n                      (try (.unlinkSync ^js fs own-path)\n                           (catch :default _ nil)))]\n       (.closeSync ^js fs (.openSync ^js fs own-path \"wx\"))\n       (try\n         (when claimed (claimed))\n         (let [live (into []\n                          (remove\n                            (fn [[file-name pid]]\n                              (when-not (live-pid? pid)\n                                (try (.unlinkSync ^js fs (str dir \"/\" file-name))\n                                     (catch :default _ nil))\n                                true)))\n                          (remove #(= own (first %)) (claim-entries fs dir)))]\n           (when (seq live)\n             (throw (ex-info (str dir \" is locked by another process\"\n                                  \" (claims: \"\n                                  (str/join \", \" (map first live)) \")\")\n                             {:dir dir :claims (mapv first live)})))\n           release!)\n         (catch :default e\n           (release!)\n           (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defonce ^:private held\n  ;; The host-ownership exception to the no-hidden-global-state invariant\n  ;; (see the namespace docstring): the canonical paths of the durable\n  ;; directories this process holds locked.\n  (atom #{}))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-dir\n  \"The directory's one canonical path: the key of this process's own\n   holdings, so two spellings of one directory are one owner.\"\n  [dir]\n  #?(:cljd (.resolveSymbolicLinksSync (dart-io/Directory. dir))\n     :clj (.getCanonicalPath (java.io.File. ^String dir))\n     :cljs (.realpathSync ^js (js/require \"fs\") dir)))\n\n\n(defn- claim!\n  #?(:clj (let [f (java.io.File. path)]\n            (when-let [parent (.getParentFile f)] (.mkdirs parent))\n            (java.io.RandomAccessFile. f \"rw\"))\n     :cljs (if-not (exists? js/require)\n             (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing.file requires the Node.js fs module.\"\n                             {:path path}))\n             (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")\n                   path-module (js/require \"path\")\n                   dir (.dirname path-module path)]\n               (when-not (.existsSync fs dir)\n                 (.mkdirSync fs dir #js {:recursive true}))\n               {:fs fs, :fd (.openSync fs path \"a+\")}))\n     :cljd (let [f (dart-io/File. path)\n                 dir (.-parent f)]\n             (when-not (.existsSync dir) (.createSync dir .recursive true))\n             (.openSync f .mode dart-io/FileMode.append))))\n\n\n(defn- file-length!\n  [f]\n  #?(:clj (.length ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f)\n     :cljs (.-size (.fstatSync ^js (:fs f) (:fd f)))\n     :cljd (.lengthSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f)))\n\n\n(defn- read-int!\n  \"The 4-byte big-endian signed frame length at offset.\"\n  [f offset]\n  #?(:clj (do (.seek ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f offset)\n              (.readInt ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f))\n     :cljs (let [head (js/Buffer.alloc prefix-size)]\n             (.readSync ^js (:fs f) (:fd f) head 0 prefix-size offset)\n             (.readInt32BE head 0))\n     :cljd (do (.setPositionSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f offset)\n               (let [^typed/Uint8List bs (.readSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f\n                                                    prefix-size)]\n                 (.getInt32 (typed/ByteData.view (.-buffer bs)) 0)))))\n\n\n(defn- read-bytes!\n  \"The len bytes at offset as the host byte array the codec accepts.\"\n  [f offset len]\n  #?(:clj (do (.seek ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f offset)\n              (let [b (byte-array len)]\n                (.readFully ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f b)\n                b))\n     :cljs (let [buf (js/Buffer.alloc len)]\n             (.readSync ^js (:fs f) (:fd f) buf 0 len offset)\n             (js/Uint8Array. buf))\n     :cljd (do (.setPositionSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f offset)\n               (.readSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f len))))\n\n\n(defn- truncate-file!\n  [f offset]\n  #?(:clj (.setLength ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f offset)\n     :cljs (.ftruncateSync ^js (:fs f) (:fd f) offset)\n     :cljd (.truncateSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f offset)))\n\n\n(defn- append-frame!\n  \"Write the length-prefixed frame at the current end of the file.\"\n  [f frame]\n  #?(:clj (let [raf ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f\n                len (alength ^bytes frame)]\n            (.seek raf (.length raf))\n            (.writeInt raf len)\n            (.write raf ^bytes frame))\n     :cljs (let [{:keys [fs fd]} f\n                 len (.-length ^js frame)\n                 head (js/Buffer.alloc prefix-size)]\n             (.writeInt32BE head len 0)\n             (.writeSync ^js fs fd head)\n             (.writeSync ^js fs fd frame))\n     :cljd (let [raf ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f\n                 len (.-length ^typed/Uint8List frame)\n                 head (typed/ByteData. prefix-size)]\n             (.setInt32 head 0 len)\n             (.setPositionSync raf (.lengthSync raf))\n             (.writeFromSync raf (.asUint8List (.-buffer head)))\n             (.writeFromSync raf frame))))\n\n\n(defn- sync-file!\n  \"Flush the frame just written to durable storage (F1: a put is\n   acknowledged only after this).\"\n  [f]\n  #?(:clj (.sync (.getFD ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f))\n     :cljs (.fsyncSync ^js (:fs f) (:fd f))\n     :cljd (.flushSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f)))\n\n\n(defn- close-file!\n  [f]\n  #?(:clj (.close ^java.io.RandomAccessFile f)\n     :cljs (.closeSync ^js (:fs f) (:fd f))\n     :cljd (.closeSync ^dart-io/RandomAccessFile f)))\n\nrg: test/dao/data/arrays_test.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:15:   - `dao.jing/sha256-bytes`, `dao.jing/canonical-bytes`, and\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:20:     test/resources/dao/jing/blake3-vectors.edn and\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:51:(def ^:private blake3-vectors*\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:53:           (read-text \"test/resources/dao/jing/blake3-vectors.edn\"))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:61:(defn- blake3-vectors\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:63:  @blake3-vectors*)\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:72:;; line: [1 2 3] under blake3 (the digest-table fixture), the empty input\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:73:;; under sha256, and a blake3 digest one nibble off.\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:74:(def ^:private blake3-digest\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:78:(def ^:private sha256-digest\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:86:(def ^:private blake3-address\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:87:  (keyword \"segment\" (str \"blake3-\" blake3-digest)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:90:(def ^:private sha256-address\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:91:  (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" sha256-digest)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:95:  (keyword \"segment\" (str \"blake3-\" mismatch-digest)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:131:  (testing \"blake3 and sha256 are the two pinned canonical forms\"\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:132:    (is (= {:algorithm :blake3\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:133:            :digest blake3-digest\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:134:            :canonical blake3-address}\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:135:           (parse-segment-address blake3-address)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:136:    (is (= {:algorithm :sha256\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:137:            :digest sha256-digest\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:138:            :canonical sha256-address}\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:139:           (parse-segment-address sha256-address)))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:146:    (is (= :blake3 (segment-algorithm blake3-address)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:147:    (is (= blake3-digest (segment-digest blake3-address)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:160:    (doseq [bad [\"Blake3\" \"sha-256\" \"\" \"sha_256\" \"blake3!\"]]\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:168:             (:digest (parse-segment-address (segment \"sha256\" digest))))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:175:                (keyword \"invalid\" (str \"sha256-\" sixty-four-a)))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:176:    (is (nil? (parse-segment-address :segment/sha256)))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:185:                (segment \"sha256\" (str/upper-case sixty-four-a))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:188:                (segment \"sha256\" (apply str (repeat 63 \"a\"))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:190:                (segment \"sha256\" (apply str (repeat 65 \"a\"))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:191:    (is (nil? (parse-segment-address :segment/sha256-1234))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:194:                (segment \"sha256\" (str (apply str (repeat 63 \"a\")) \"g\"))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:202:      (is (= (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" digest))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:204:                           (segment \"sha256\" digest))))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:238:(deftest segment-matches-total-predicate-blake3-and-sha256\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:239:  (testing \"a genuine blake3-addressed payload matches, using the frozen\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:241:    (is (segment-matches? blake3-address [1 2 3])))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:242:  (testing \"a genuine sha256-addressed payload matches, using the real\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:244:    (let [address (jing/segment-key {:a 1} {:algorithm :sha256})]\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:258:                  (segment \"sha256\"\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:262:    (let [address (segment \"sha256\" sixty-four-a)]\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:282:    (doseq [address [blake3-address sha256-address]]\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:416:  (testing \"blake3-vectors.edn parses with the expected case counts\"\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:417:    (is (= 10 (count (:official-vectors (blake3-vectors)))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:418:    (is (= 5 (count (:non-ascii-utf8-vectors (blake3-vectors))))))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:437:(deftest blake3-official-and-utf8-vector-conformance\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:439:    (let [vectors (:official-vectors (blake3-vectors))]\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:443:          (is (= hash (jing/digest-bytes :blake3 input-bytes))\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:446:    (let [vectors (:non-ascii-utf8-vectors (blake3-vectors))]\ntest/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:449:        (is (= hash (jing/digest-string :blake3 string))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:4:   table and NFC seam, §2's root framing and input validation, §3's\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:126:    (is (= :blake3 (get-in dimension [:dim/encoding :hash])))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:132:    (is (= (jing/blake3 (d/encode-value d/descriptor)) d/dimension-hash)\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:201:    (is (= composed (d/normalize-nfc decomposed)))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:202:    (is (= composed (d/normalize-nfc composed)))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:203:    (is (= \"abc\" (d/normalize-nfc \"abc\")))))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:783:;; D2 fix round: preimage-keyed consing, NFC idents, verified content address\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:812:        \"ident parts pass the NFC seam: one free name, one hash\")))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:853:;; the essay fingerprint below is the stable §8 row. (A live pair transport —\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:912:      \"two string keys with one NFC form collide\")\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:923:      \"the NFC limit: a decomposed string and its composed form are one\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:932:(deftest the-essays-fingerprint-is-stable\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:963:        \"the decomposed input's record carries the composed NFC spelling\")))\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:1248:        \"two spellings of é are one NFC element\")\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:1364:         literals, nil, a true :macro?, sets, doubles, NaN, and NFC keys\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:1544:(deftest continuation-markers-are-distinct-and-stable\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc:1551:          \"stable under repeated projection and shuffled input\"))\n(ns yin.vm.linker.sign-test\n  (:require [clojure.edn :as edn]\n            [clojure.test :refer [deftest is]]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yin.vm.linker.authority :as authority]\n            [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]\n            #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]])))\n\n\n(defn- vectors\n  []\n  (edn/read-string\n    #?(:cljd (.readAsStringSync\n               (dart-io/File. \"test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn\"))\n       :clj (slurp \"test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn\")\n       :cljs (.readFileSync (js/require \"fs\")\n                            \"test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn\" \"utf8\"))))\n\n\n(defn- hex\n  [bytes]\n  (apply str (mapcat (fn [b]\n                       (let [n (bit-and b 255)\n                             digits \"0123456789abcdef\"]\n                         [(nth digits (quot n 16))\n                          (nth digits (mod n 16))])) bytes)))\n\n\n(deftest rfc-8032-vectors\n  (doseq [{:keys [seed public message signature]} (:rfc (vectors))]\n    (is (= public (sign/public-of seed)))\n    (is (= signature (sign/sign-message seed message)))))\n\n\n(deftest canonical-envelope-vectors\n  (let [v (vectors)\n        seed (get-in v [:rfc 0 :seed])\n        public (get-in v [:rfc 0 :public])]\n    (doseq [case-key [:assertion :retraction]]\n      (let [{:keys [envelope canonical message signature id]}\n            (get v case-key)]\n        (is (= canonical (hex (jing/canonical-bytes envelope))))\n        (is (= message (str \"79696e2e6d6f64756c652f656e76656c6f70653a76310a\"\n                            canonical)))\n        (is (= signature\n               (:yin.module/signature (sign/sign-envelope seed envelope))))\n        (is (= id (jing/segment-key envelope)))\n        (is (sign/verify-envelope public (jing/canonical-bytes envelope)\n                                  signature))))))\n\n\n(defn- bad-proof?\n  [env proof public]\n  (let [result (authority/name-environment\n                 {:snapshot :test\n                  :principals\n                  {(:yin.module/asserted-by env)\n                   {:proof :yin.module/signature\n                    :key public :verify sign/verify-envelope :seq-floor 0}}}\n                 [{:yin.module/envelope env :yin.module/proof proof}])]\n    (some #(= :bad-proof (:kind %)) (:diagnostics result))))\n\n\n(defn- resolution\n  [env proof public]\n  (authority/name-environment\n    {:snapshot :test\n     :principals {(:yin.module/asserted-by env)\n                  {:proof :yin.module/signature\n                   :key public :verify sign/verify-envelope :seq-floor 0}}}\n    [{:yin.module/envelope env :yin.module/proof proof}]))\n\n\n(deftest tampering-is-bad-proof\n  (let [v (vectors)\n        env (get-in v [:assertion :envelope])\n        proof {:yin.module/signature\n               (get-in v [:assertion :signature])}\n        public (get-in v [:rfc 0 :public])\n        t (:tamper v)]\n    (is (= :ok (get-in (resolution env proof public)\n                       [:names 'my.lib :status])))\n    (doseq [tampered [(assoc env :yin.module/name (:name t))\n                      (assoc env :yin.module/manifest (:manifest t))\n                      (assoc env :yin.module/seq (:seq t))\n                      {:yin.module/op :retract\n                       :yin.module/of (jing/segment-key env)\n                       :yin.module/asserted-by (:yin.module/asserted-by env)\n                       :yin.module/seq (:yin.module/seq env)}\n                      (assoc env :yin.module/asserted-by\n                             (sign/principal (:wrong-public t)))]]\n      (is (bad-proof? tampered proof public)))\n    (is (bad-proof? env\n                    {:yin.module/signature\n                     (:bad-signature t)}\n                    public))\n    (is (bad-proof? env proof (:wrong-public t)))\n    (is (bad-proof? (assoc env :yin.module/asserted-by\n                           (sign/principal (:wrong-public t)))\n                    proof (:wrong-public t)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The review has blocking findings: H2 deletes a namespace still required by several consumers, the proposed profile omits carrier operations, and H0’s grep gate cannot prove the claimed boundary. The scheduler change also preserves a callback interface that ABI §7 explicitly rejects.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '145,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '395,440p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '540,625p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.abi.md | tail -12; sed -n '1,55p' src/cljc/yin/repl/store.cljc; rg -n '\\\\(defn|\\\\(deftest' src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc; sed -n '710,735p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '145,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '395,440p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md | sed -n '540,625p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.abi.md | tail -12; sed -n '1,55p' src/cljc/yin/repl/store.cljc; rg -n '\\\\(defn|\\\\(deftest' src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc test/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc; sed -n '710,735p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":"   145\t  handed the deposit operation and its own host resource.\n   146\t\n   147\tSo a host dependency is one of two kinds, and the design treats them\n   148\tdifferently:\n   149\t\n   150\t+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   151\t| Kind                           | Definition                                                         | Target shape                                                     |\n   152\t+================================+====================================================================+==================================================================+\n   153\t| **World fact or effect**       | Something shipped code cannot compute: a socket, a file, a lock, a | A `dao.stream` implementation: portable core plus a host seam    |\n   154\t|                                | clock reading, entropy, another process's parser output.           | value the composition supplies (§4). The seam is the only host   |\n   155\t|                                |                                                                    | code. This is the owner's \"only dao.stream implementations\".     |\n   156\t+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   157\t| **Machine-internal carrier or  | A value kind the dialect itself defines (exact integer, float,     | One **primitive profile**: an enumerable set of functions with a |\n   158\t| pure function over carriers**  | string, byte array, host array) or a deterministic function over   | portable oracle (frozen vectors or corpora) per entry (§4.3).    |\n   159\t|                                | such values (hashing, signing, canonical encoding, NFC).           | Never a stream: a stream round trip per integer addition is not  |\n   160\t|                                |                                                                    | an abstraction, it is a 10^3-10^5 slowdown of the inner loop.    |\n   161\t+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   162\t\n   163\t**The hot-path line, stated.** Anything invoked per VM step, per guest\n   164\tvalue, per encoded byte or per content address is machine-internal and stays\n   165\ta function. Anything invoked per I/O event (an open, a bind, a lock, a\n   166\treceived datagram, a parsed source unit) is a stream operation. The\n   167\tobservable test: if the operation's answer could be different on a different\n   168\tmachine at a different time, it is a world fact and crosses a stream; if its\n   169\tanswer is a function of its inputs alone, it is a primitive and has an oracle.\n   170\t\n   171\tThis refines, and does not contradict, `datom.world.md:70`. A primitive\n   172\tprofile adds no contract to `dao.stream` and is not a second ABI: it is the\n   173\tdialect's value model made explicit and testable, which `datom.world.abi.md`\n   174\t§3 already presupposes (\"in-machine values\"). What the owner's sentence\n   175\tachieves under this reading: **a port implements stream seams; the primitive\n   176\tprofile is the port's value model, verified by vectors, not designed per\n   177\thost.**\n   178\t\n   179\t## 3. Classification of every kind of host dependency\n   180\t\n   395\tof its own (enforced by the grep gate, §8 H0).\n   396\t\n   397\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   398\t| Namespace (P)         | Entries (consolidated from, V)                                  | Count   | Oracle (V where it exists)                              |\n   399\t+=======================+=================================================================+=========+=========================================================+\n   400\t| `dao.host.integer`    | `yin.vm.integer.host` entire (recognition x3, float predicates  | ~20     | `test/yin/vm/integer_v3_fixtures.cljc` and               |\n   401\t|                       | x3, promote/demote/from-native x4, 13 big operations) absorbing |         | `integer_test.cljc` (26 deftests); the cbor corpus for  |\n   402\t|                       | `cbor.cljc:106-254` (`big`, `b+`, `b-`, `b*`, `bquot`, `bcmp`,  |         | >64-bit integers and magnitude bytes.                   |\n   403\t|                       | `bgcd`, `b->int`, `magnitude-bytes`, bit length).               |         |                                                         |\n   404\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   405\t| `dao.host.float`      | `float64->bits`, `bits->float64`, `nan?`, `infinite?`,          | ~5      | cbor corpus float cases (`7ff8...` NaN rule); de Bruijn |\n   406\t|                       | `finite?` (from `cbor.cljc:525-560`, `debruijn.cljc:855-885`,   |         | scalar fixtures.                                        |\n   407\t|                       | `integer/host.cljc:86-108`, `data.cljc:77-92`).                 |         |                                                         |\n   408\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   409\t| `dao.host.bytes`      | `bytes?`, `alloc`, `length`, `get`, `set!`, `slice`, `copy!`,   | ~9      | Property tests (round trip, slice laws) and the cbor    |\n   410\t|                       | `equal?`, `from-vector` (from `chunks`, `jing.dht`, `linker`,   |         | corpus (byte-string cases).                             |\n   411\t|                       | `sign`, `datagram`, `codec`, `cbor/boring`).                    |         |                                                         |\n   412\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   413\t| `dao.host.text`       | `unit-count`, `code-unit`, `units->string`, `utf8-encode`,      | ~6      | `data_test` code-point cases; cbor corpus               |\n   414\t|                       | `utf8-decode`, `nfc` (from `data.cljc:119-173`, `debruijn:241,  |         | `unpaired-surrogate` refusals; Unicode NFC vectors for  |\n   415\t|                       | 319`, `jing.cljc`, `sign.cljc:62-66`).                          |         | `nfc`.                                                  |\n   416\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   417\t| `dao.host.hash`       | `blake3-bytes`, `sha256-bytes` (from `jing.cljc:59-135`; string | 2       | Official BLAKE3 and SHA-256 vectors (the spike checked   |\n   418\t|                       | forms become portable over `text`).                             |         | three, R); `hash_registry_contract_test`; every Jing    |\n   419\t|                       |                                                                 |         | address in the corpus.                                  |\n   420\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   421\t| `dao.host.sign`       | `keypair-from-seed`, `sign`, `verify` (from `sign.cljc`; hex,   | 3       | RFC 8032 vectors and `sign_vectors.edn` (V:              |\n   422\t|                       | domains, message assembly already portable).                    |         | `sign_test.cljc`, 5 deftests).                          |\n   423\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   424\t| `dao.host.array`      | `dao.data.arrays` as is (`anew`, `aget`, `aset`, `alength`,     | ~10     | btree fixtures (`psset_fixtures`, `btree_test`); the    |\n   425\t|                       | `acopy`, `aclone`, `asort` made stable, ...).                   |         | sort entry's order is pinned by a fixture.              |\n   426\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   427\t| Portable, no seam     | base64 (over `bytes`), the Jing CBOR writer (over `bytes`,      | 0       | `base64` tests; `cbor-v1.json`, `cbor_fixtures`,        |\n   428\t|                       | `integer`, `float`, `text`), hex.                               |         | `cbor_conformance_test`.                                |\n   429\t+-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------------------+\n   430\t\n   431\tAbout 55 functions. Each entry's docstring states its law on every host (as\n   432\t`integer/host.cljc` does today, V), and the profile has one manifest test\n   433\tnamespace, `dao.host.conformance-test` (P), that runs every oracle above on\n   434\tevery lane. A host that cannot provide an entry natively implements it in\n   435\tportable code over the other entries (bit operations over arithmetic;\n   436\t`asort` as a mergesort); the oracle does not care how.\n   437\t\n   438\tLayering: `dao.host.*` requires nothing in the repo. `dao.jing` and\n   439\t`yin.vm.integer` require it; `yin.vm.integer.host` is deleted after its\n   440\tconsumers move (no backward compatibility is owed in this repo). Dependency\n   540\t|       |        | gains one. Record per-lane counts. Record the Jing corpus hash set.            | (one negative test). Counts recorded.                             |\n   541\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   542\t| H1    | expand | Create `dao.host.integer`, `dao.host.float`, `dao.host.bytes`, `dao.host.text` | Three lanes green; counts unchanged plus `dao.host.conformance-   |\n   543\t|       |        | with today's arms moved verbatim, plus `dao.host.conformance-test` running the | test` named; cbor corpus bytes identical; `integer_v3_fixtures`   |\n   544\t|       |        | existing oracles against them. No consumer changes.                            | unchanged. Allowlist grows by the four files.                     |\n   545\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   546\t| H2    | migr.  | `yin.vm.integer` and `dao.jing.cbor` require `dao.host.integer`/`float`;       | Green; counts unchanged; corpus identical; `grep BigInteger       |\n   547\t|       |        | `cbor.cljc:106-254` and `yin.vm.integer.host` deleted. `chunks`, `jing.dht`,   | src/cljc` hits only `dao/host/integer.cljc`; allowlist shrinks by |\n   548\t|       |        | `linker`, `sign`, `datagram`, `codec`, `data`, `debruijn` read `bytes`/`text`. | the migrated files.                                               |\n   549\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   550\t| H3    | expand | `dao.host.hash` and `dao.host.sign`; `dao.jing` and `yin.vm.linker.sign`       | Green; counts unchanged plus the vector tests named; every Jing   |\n   551\t|       | +migr. | require them; their arms deleted. Base64 made portable over `bytes`.           | address in the corpus identical; `sign_vectors.edn` passes;       |\n   552\t|       |        |                                                                                | `grep MessageDigest\\|Blake3\\|noble\\|ed25519 src/cljc` hits only   |\n   553\t|       |        |                                                                                | `dao/host/`.                                                      |\n   554\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   555\t| H4    | migr.  | Portable Jing CBOR writer over `dao.host.*`; `dao.jing.cbor` requires it on    | Green; `cbor-v1.json`, `cbor_fixtures`, `cbor_conformance_test`   |\n   556\t|       |        | every host; `dao.jing.cbor.boring` and `dao.jing.cbor.cljd` deleted; the       | byte-identical on three lanes; the frozen DHT and linker image    |\n   557\t|       |        | Boring dependency stays only for `dao.stream.cbor.boring` (codec track's).     | goldens unchanged; a write-throughput measurement recorded        |\n   558\t|       |        |                                                                                | against Boring on the JVM (informational; not a gate).            |\n   559\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   560\t| H5    | migr.  | Entropy and scheduler to composition parameters: `dao.space.dht` and           | Green; counts unchanged; `grep SecureRandom\\|getRandomValues\\|    |\n   561\t|       |        | `yin.vm.linker.sign` take `random-bytes`; `dao.jing.content.async` takes       | setTimeout src/cljc` hits only `yin/repl/main.cljc` (the driver)  |\n   562\t|       |        | `schedule`; `yin.repl.main` supplies them.                                     | or nothing.                                                       |\n   563\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   564\t|       |        | **Phase A boundary: slow tier once. Everything above is pure code motion.**     |                                                                   |\n   565\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   566\t| H6    | expand | The durable-storage seam (§4.2): `dao.host.storage` per host under `src/clj`,  | Green; `file_test` (18), `fs_test` (4), btree durability, journal |\n   567\t|       | +migr. | `src/cljs`, `src/cljd`; `dao.jing.file`, `dao.space.store.fs`, `store`,        | and transactor suites unchanged in count and result; kill/restart |\n   568\t|       |        | `journal.file`, `btree.storage` portable over a seam value the composition     | and competing-process fixtures (R: named in the storage           |\n   569\t|       |        | passes. Largest slice; split per namespace, `jing.file` first.                 | docstrings) still pass; the seam's capability map replaces        |\n   570\t|       |        |                                                                                | `directory-sync-supported?`. Allowlist loses five files.          |\n   571\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   572\t| H7    | expand | Parser stage as a declared seam value; one test composes the stage on one      | Green; counts unchanged plus the remote-stage test; packet        |\n   573\t|       |        | side of an in-memory `dao.stream` and the lowering on the other, proving the   | goldens unchanged.                                                |\n   574\t|       |        | stage is relocatable without code change.                                      |                                                                   |\n   575\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   576\t| H8    | expand | `dao.stream.conformance-test` over a scripted seam, run against ws, datagram,  | Green; one named suite; each implementation's declared outcome    |\n   577\t|       |        | journal and the in-memory implementations; `yin.vm.engine.md` §E.5 gains a     | subset documented and asserted; doc cross-link.                   |\n   578\t|       |        | one-paragraph pointer to §4.5.                                                 |                                                                   |\n   579\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   580\t|       |        | **Phase B boundary: slow tier once; H0's allowlist is now `dao/host/*` plus    |                                                                   |\n   581\t|       |        | the seam files only.**                                                         |                                                                   |\n   582\t+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   583\t\n   584\tDependencies: H1 before H2, H2 before H3 and H4 (both need `bytes`), H5\n   585\tindependent after H1; H6 after H2 (bytes) and H3 (addresses), H7 and H8\n   586\tindependent after H0. H0-H5 touch none of `dao.stream.ws`, `.transit`,\n   587\t`.cbor`, `.cbor.boring`, `.codec`, `remote_channel`, the four ws adapters or\n   588\t`yin.repl.serve/connect`; the codec track's C2-C4 edit exactly those, so the\n   589\ttwo tracks are file-disjoint and may run concurrently. H2's one touch of\n   590\t`dao.stream.codec` (reading `host-bytes?` through `dao.host.bytes`) is\n   591\tdeferred until the codec track's C4 has landed, or done by that track in C4\n   592\tif it prefers; either way it is one require. The codec track is **not**\n   593\tabsorbed: it owns the wire codec; this document owns the host floor beneath\n   594\tevery codec.\n   595\t\n   596\tRisk to the three lanes: low for H1-H5 (code motion behind byte oracles;\n   597\tthe known traps are ClojureDart's `:clj` reader feature, so every new file\n   598\tnames `:cljd` first, and cljd's protocol this-param cast). Medium for H6: the\n   599\tstorage namespaces are the least tested across hosts (R: the spike never\n   600\texercised them) and Node's claim-file lock has process-liveness semantics\n   601\tthat must be reproduced exactly behind the seam; split per namespace and\n   602\tkeep the existing fixtures as the gate. H4 risk is bounded by the corpus:\n   603\teither the bytes match or the slice does not land.\n   604\t\n   605\t## 9. Cost and what would reverse this\n   606\t\n   607\tCost, orders of magnitude (P, from the §1 census):\n   608\t- Files edited: ~10^1 for phase A (about 20 of 183 portable files, most by\n   609\t  a require change and arm deletion; 4-6 new `dao.host.*` files); ~10^1\n   610\t  more for phase B (five storage files, three new seam files, two test\n   611\t  suites). Nothing in `yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.semantic.*`, `dao.space.index`\n   612\t  or `dao.space.query` is touched.\n   613\t- Tests: rerun ~10^3 per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000 tests; the\n   614\t  `dao.stream` and `dao.jing` subsystems about 690 per lane); rewritten ~10^1\n   615\t  (the tests that construct storage or sign keys directly, and\n   616\t  `integer_test`'s shim cases, which move with the shim); added ~10^1.\n   617\t\n   618\tWhat would reverse the design:\n   619\t- An owner ruling that reader-conditional arms in portable files are the\n   620\t  intended port mechanism (a port is then \"add a feature arm in ~15 files\",\n   621\t  which the spike showed is about 150 lines, R). That is cheaper per port\n   622\t  and leaves no enumeration, no oracle per entry, and a library requirement\n   623\t  per host; this design's value is the enumeration, not fewer lines.\n   624\t- A measured regression in H4 (the portable Jing writer) that matters on a\n   625\t  hot path. Then the writer stays portable as the oracle and a host may\n   138\t(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n   139\tdoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n   140\tnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n   141\tinterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n   142\t\n   143\tTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n   144\tcall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n   145\tappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n   146\tcallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n   147\tthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n   148\tdirections wearing the shape of an event.\n   149\t\n(ns yin.repl.store\n  \"Startup selection of the code index's store\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md; the durable-store startup\n   contract, slices 1-3, and the DHT store of epic S5).\n\n   The store is chosen once, before the shell composes: `mem` — a fresh\n   in-memory `dao.jing` store, today's behaviour — `file:<dir>`, the\n   durable directory store (dao.space.store), or `dht:<dir>`, that store\n   with a DHT node composed over it (yin.repl.dht).  This namespace parses\n   the `--index-store` value, validates the `:index-store-spec` an\n   embedder may hand `yin.repl/create-state`, and opens the store the\n   resolved spec names.  Every bad spec — a missing value, an unknown\n   scheme, an empty directory, a directory that cannot be opened, a host\n   build without file support — is refused with its reason; nothing falls\n   back to memory silently.\n\n   The durable directory itself — the exclusive lock, HEAD, the validated\n   snapshot, `:recovery` — is dao.space.store's; this namespace is one of\n   its consumers.  The shell installs the recovery before it admits any\n   evaluation (`yin.repl.index/rehydrate`), and a durable store\n   (`durable?`) also carries the index across `(reset)` and VM\n   selection; the memory store keeps today's empty rebuild.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.mem :as jing.mem]\n            [dao.space.store :as durable]\n            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram]))\n\n\n(def file-scheme\n  \"The `--index-store` scheme prefix naming a durable file store.\"\n  \"file:\")\n\n\n(def dht-scheme\n  \"The `--index-store` scheme prefix naming the DHT store: the durable\n   directory store of `file:<dir>` composed under a `dao.jing.dht` node\n   (yin.repl.dht).\"\n  \"dht:\")\n\n\n(def ^:private supported-args\n  [\"mem\" (str file-scheme \"<dir>\") (str dht-scheme \"<dir>\")])\n\n\n(defn parse-arg\n  \"The `--index-store` value as a spec: `:mem`, or `{:type :file :dir dir}`\n   for `file:<dir>`.  A missing value, an unknown scheme, and an empty\n   directory are refused here — with the supported forms named — so the\n   arguments parser can refuse a bad flag before anything composes.\"\n  [value]\n  (cond\n    (nil? value)\n    (throw (ex-info \"--index-store needs a value: mem, file:<dir> or dht:<dir>\"\n                    {:supported supported-args}))\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:28:(defn anew\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:40:(defn aget\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:50:(defn aset\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:60:(defn alength\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:69:(defn aclone\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:76:(defn acopy\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:108:(defn asub\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:123:(defn aconcat\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:138:(defn asort\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:151:(defn ainsert-at\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:167:(defn aremove-at\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:182:(defn from-coll\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:44:(defn- byte-hex\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:50:(defn- code-at\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:57:(defn- ascii?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:62:(defn- ascii-hex\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:67:(defn- ascii-bytes\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:72:(defn- text-item\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:78:(defn- refusal-of\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:90:(defn- check!\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:98:(defn- guarded\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:107:(defn- assert-none!\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:114:(defn- assert-none-of\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:161:(defn- decimal-exponent-ok?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:172:(defn- stop!\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:177:(defn- arg-of\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:182:(defn- read-head\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:215:(defn- int-node?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:221:(defn- null-node?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:226:(defn- frame-kind\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:236:(defn- strictly-ascending?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:241:(defn- magnitude\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:248:(defn- magnitudes\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:255:(defn- gcd\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:260:(defn- check-frame!\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:313:(defn- check-tag!\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:353:(defn- read-item\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:425:(defn walk\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:458:(defn- errata-rows\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:484:(defn errata-merged\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:501:(defn documented-skips\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:507:(defn- skipped-anywhere\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:516:(defn- refusal-name\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:521:(defn- canonical-entry\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:541:(defn- encode-refusal-entry\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:564:(defn- decode-refusal-entry\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:579:(defn build-manifest\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:605:(defn manifest\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:614:(defn- resource-entry\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:627:(defn- common-ids\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:635:(defn- digest-lines\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:644:(defn- digest-of\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:651:(defn- resource-entries\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:671:(deftest manifest-agrees-with-the-resource-for-every-case-this-host-runs\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:675:(deftest every-case-is-run-or-skipped-by-a-documented-id\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:702:(deftest documented-skips-match-the-errata-and-leave-no-case-unrun\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:783:(deftest canonical-manifest-digest-equals-the-value-derived-from-the-resource\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:797:(deftest refusal-manifest-digest-equals-the-value-derived-from-the-resource\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:814:(deftest produced-bytes-pass-the-raw-cbor-inspection\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:822:(deftest the-frozen-canonical-bytes-pass-the-raw-cbor-inspection\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:830:(defn- flagged?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:835:(deftest the-walker-flags-what-it-must\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:891:(deftest the-walker-independently-flags-the-refusal-classes-it-can-see\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:909:(deftest injectivity-and-groups-hold-on-the-bytes-this-host-produced\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:951:(deftest the-resource-bytes-are-the-frozen-bytes\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:986:     (defn- scan-files\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:997:     (defn- shell-like?\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:1002:     (defn- offences\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:1022:     (deftest no-source-file-that-names-the-resource-writes-files\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:1032:     (deftest the-scan-flags-write-attempts-it-must-catch\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:1055:     (deftest the-generator-cannot-overwrite-the-resource\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc:1066:     (defn- resource-pin-holds?\na tail-position `:cljd nil` silently fails; protocol method parameters must\nnot be `[_ _]`; `(- 0.0)` is `+0.0`; multi-key `assoc` on nil misbehaves.\n\nReview requirement: this design and any implementation an orchestrator\nderives from it under a Claude model are Claude-authored. Sign-off on the\ndesign and on each slice must come from a non-Claude architect; a Claude\nreviewer may review but may not be the sole sign-off. The reviewer checks, in\norder: that no slice deletes an arm a later slice still needs (expand before\ncontract); that every H1-H4 acceptance is byte-identical against the named\ncorpus on all three lanes; that the allowlist shrinks monotonically and the\nnegative test still fails; that H6 preserves the Node claim-file liveness\nrule and the HEAD rename atomicity exactly; that nothing in this track edits\na codec-track file while that track's C2-C4 are in flight.\n\n## Appendix A. Citations opened for this document (2026-10-20, read only)\n\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Cite                                                         | Finding                                                                                          |\n+==============================================================+==================================================================================================+\n| `docs/design/datom.world.md:16-32,43-48,68-71`               | Axioms; streams; \"A host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary\".                       |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/datom.world.abi.md:21-24,35-44,47-52,56-64,`    | One ABI, many implementations; custody; machine-internal work inside the instance; the floor is  |\n| `83-87,94-148`                                               | world facts; effects out, events in; adapter discipline; \"two things are not adapters\".          |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `docs/design/dao.stream.wasm.md:14-27,29-52,96-105`          | Import surface is one implementation; in-instance ring buffer needs no crossing; Jing bytes.     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/chunks.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/base64.cljc; sed -n '1,70p' src/clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj; sed -n '1,48p' src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj; sed -n '95,125p' src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc; sed -n '137,154p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc; sed -n '621,652p' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc; sed -n '1,68p' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc; sed -n '1,110p' src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/chunks.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/base64.cljc; sed -n '1,70p' src/clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj; sed -n '1,48p' src/clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj; sed -n '95,125p' src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc; sed -n '137,154p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc; sed -n '621,652p' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc; sed -n '1,68p' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc; sed -n '1,110p' src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.repl.host\n  \"The one seam between the Yin REPL and a real host WebSocket package.\n\n   `yin.repl.connect` and `yin.repl.serve` are complete compositions over\n   the DaoStream WebSocket boundary, which deliberately knows no socket\n   library: `dao.stream.ws/make-attacher` asks a host for `:connect!`, and a\n   listener is likewise host policy.  Each supported build composes that host\n   policy behind the same `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}` value: this portable\n   namespace selects the JVM adapter, while host-specific `.cljs` and `.cljd`\n   shadows select Node and dart:io.\n\n   A shadow replaces this namespace wholesale, so it holds only `websocket` —\n   the one thing that differs per build.  The portable contract every build\n   shares lives in `yin.repl.host.common`.\"\n  (:require #?@(:cljd []\n                :clj [[yin.repl.host.jvm :as jvm]]\n                :default [])))\n\n\n(defn websocket\n  \"Return this build's host WebSocket adapter.\n\n   A conforming adapter is a map of:\n\n   * `:connect!` — `(fn [descriptor adapter] …)`, per\n     `dao.stream.ws/make-attacher`: it starts connecting, never waits, and\n     synchronously returns `{:send! … :close! …}`;\n   * `:bind!` — `(fn [{:keys [endpoint bind-host bind-port path accept! deposit!]}] …)`,\n     which binds a listener, routes each upgrade through\n     `(accept! request-path socket now)` with its own clock reading, and reports\n     every lifecycle fact through `deposit!` as plain data;\n   * `:unbind!` — `(fn [resources deposit!] …)`, which releases the listener and\n     whose close completion deposits `:stopped`.\"\n  []\n  #?(:cljd nil\n     :clj (jvm/websocket)\n     :default nil))\n(ns dao.stream.chunks\n  \"Transport-neutral splitting and bounded reassembly of host byte payloads.\"\n  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])))\n\n\n(defn length\n  [bs]\n  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)\n     :clj (alength ^bytes bs)\n     :cljs (.-length bs)))\n\n\n(defn empty-bytes\n  [n]\n  #?(:cljd (Uint8List. n)\n     :clj (byte-array n)\n     :cljs (js/Uint8Array. n)))\n\n\n(defn slice\n  [bs start end]\n  #?(:cljd (Uint8List.fromList (.sublist ^Uint8List bs start end))\n     :clj (java.util.Arrays/copyOfRange ^bytes bs (int start) (int end))\n     :cljs (.slice bs start end)))\n\n\n(defn concat-bytes\n  [pieces]\n  (let [out (empty-bytes (reduce + (map length pieces)))]\n    (reduce (fn [offset bs]\n              (let [n (length bs)]\n                #?(:cljd (.setRange ^Uint8List out offset (+ offset n) bs)\n                   :clj (System/arraycopy bs 0 out offset n)\n                   :cljs (.set out bs offset))\n                (+ offset n)))\n            0 pieces)\n    out))\n\n\n(defn split\n  \"Return protocol-specific envelopes fitting budget. envelope receives part,\n   parts, bytes; encode measures the actual wire size.\"\n  [payload budget envelope encode]\n  (let [total (length payload)]\n    (loop [size (min total budget)]\n      (when-not (pos? size)\n        (throw (ex-info \"chunk envelope cannot fit datagram\" {:budget budget})))\n      (let [parts (max 1 (quot (+ total (dec size)) size))]\n        (if (<= (length (encode (envelope (dec parts) parts (empty-bytes size))))\n                budget)\n          (mapv (fn [part]\n                  (let [start (* part size)]\n                    (envelope part parts\n                              (slice payload start (min total (+ start size))))))\n                (range parts))\n          (recur (dec size)))))))\n\n\n(defn absorb\n  \"Return [state completed-bytes-or-nil]. Malformed, duplicate, inconsistent,\n   and overbound pieces cannot increase held bytes. Evict oldest partial at\n   the global count bound. Caller supplies a source-qualified key.\"\n  [state key part parts bytes max-bytes max-partials]\n  (if-not (and (integer? part) (integer? parts) (pos? parts)\n               (<= parts max-bytes) (<= 0 part) (< part parts)\n               (some? bytes) (pos? (length bytes))\n               (<= (length bytes) max-bytes) (pos? max-partials))\n    [state nil]\n    (let [held (get-in state [:partial key])]\n      (if (or (and held (not= parts (:parts-count held)))\n              (and held (contains? (:parts held) part)))\n        [state nil]\n        (let [state (if held state\n                        (-> state\n                            (assoc-in [:partial key] {:parts-count parts :parts {} :bytes-total 0})\n                            (update :partial-order (fnil conj []) key)))\n              oldest (first (:partial-order state))\n              state (if (> (count (:partial state)) max-partials)\n                      (-> state (update :partial dissoc oldest)\n                          (update :partial-order #(vec (rest %)))) state)\n              held (get-in state [:partial key])]\n          (if-not held [state nil]\n                  (let [total (+ (:bytes-total held) (length bytes))]\n                    (if (> total max-bytes)\n                      [(-> state (update :partial dissoc key)\n                           (update :partial-order #(vec (remove #{key} %)))) nil]\n                      (let [state (-> state (assoc-in [:partial key :parts part] bytes)\n                                      (assoc-in [:partial key :bytes-total] total))\n                            held (get-in state [:partial key])]\n                        (if (= parts (count (:parts held)))\n                          [(-> state (update :partial dissoc key)\n                               (update :partial-order #(vec (remove #{key} %))))\n                           (concat-bytes (mapv (:parts held) (range parts)))]\n                          [state nil]))))))))))\n(ns dao.stream.base64\n  \"The strict Base64 codec of the raw datagram layer\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md 2): padded standard alphabet, no\n   whitespace, no URL-safe alphabet, the empty string for zero bytes.\n\n   This is the one codec at or below dao.stream for datagram payloads on\n   stream-visible values; it is byte-for-byte the format of dao.jing's\n   frozen bytes->base64 / base64->bytes (dao.jing.dht.md 10, Base64 seam),\n   which S3 repoints onto this namespace. Nothing here knows a datagram:\n   it is bytes in, text out, and the exact inverse under text?.\"\n\n  #?(:cljs (:require [goog.crypt.base64 :as base64]))\n  #?(:cljd (:require [\"dart:convert\" :as convert])))\n\n\n(def ^:private text-pattern\n  \"Padded standard-alphabet Base64, anchored at both ends (re-matches is\n   exec plus equality on some hosts, and an unanchored pattern would\n   answer prefixes): no whitespace, no URL-safe alphabet. A length-4k\n   tail is 4 alphabet characters, a length-4k+2 tail is two characters and\n   two pads, a length-4k+3 tail is three characters and one pad, and no\n   other shape is text.\"\n  #\"^(?:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{4})*(?:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{2}==|[A-Za-z0-9+/]{3}=)?$\")\n\n\n(defn text?\n  \"True iff `s` is strict padded standard-alphabet Base64 text, the empty\n   string included: exactly what a datagram event's :bytes field carries.\"\n  [s]\n  (and (string? s) (boolean (re-matches text-pattern s))))\n\n\n(defn encode\n  \"The Base64 text of host bytes `bs` (byte[] / Uint8Array / Uint8List):\n   the transport representation of a datagram payload on any stream, so\n   that a traffic stream survives any dao.stream codec.\"\n  [bs]\n  #?(:cljd (convert/base64Encode bs)\n     :clj (.encodeToString (java.util.Base64/getEncoder) ^bytes bs)\n     :cljs (base64/encodeByteArray bs)))\n\n\n(defn decode\n  \"The host bytes of Base64 `s`, decoded strictly -- nil, never a throw,\n   for anything that is not strict text: a layer that refuses a value\n   answers an outcome, and its codec is total like it.\"\n  [s]\n  (when (text? s)\n    #?(:cljd (try (convert/base64Decode s)\n                  (catch Object _\n                    nil))\n       :clj (.decode (java.util.Base64/getDecoder) ^String s)\n       ;; The bundled goog decoder's return shape is not guaranteed to be\n       ;; a Uint8Array on every Closure version; copy it into one, so the\n       ;; payload is the same host byte value on every host.\n       :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (base64/decodeStringToUint8Array s)))))\n(ns dao.stream.datagram.jvm\n  \"The JVM host seam of docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md 3: one\n   DatagramSocket, bound once, with a daemon receiver thread that deposits\n   every host fact and every received datagram on the composition's\n   deposit writer as plain dao.stream.datagram values. The seam takes\n   {:identity id :deposit w :bind-host h :bind-port p :max-bytes n} -- a\n   deposit writer and nothing else, no function to invoke -- and returns at\n   once {:send! f :close! f}; binding is synchronous here, so the bound\n   event is already on the deposit writer when bind! returns, and a failed\n   bind deposits bind-failed and never answers ok on send!. All protocol\n   interpretation -- validation, fragmentation, projection -- stays above\n   this seam; the transform deposits and returns, judging no datagram\n   except the max-bytes drop, which is protocol validation below it:\n   never deposited.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram])\n  (:import (java.net DatagramPacket DatagramSocket InetAddress\n                     InetSocketAddress)))\n\n\n(defn bind!\n  \"Bind one DatagramSocket per the seam contract. `:bind-host` is the\n   local interface address (the wildcard address is legal), `:bind-port` 0\n   asks for an ephemeral port, `:max-bytes` defaults to 1200 and is the\n   symmetric datagram budget. Returns {:send! f :close! f} at once:\n   `:send!` is (send! host port bytes) -> {:dao.stream/outcome\n   :dao.stream/ok} | {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   :dao.stream.datagram/reason text} (a synchronous host failure, clean:\n   nothing was sent), and every send failure is also deposited as a\n   send-failed event; `:close!` closes the host socket, deposits closed\n   while the deposit writer still can, and is idempotent.\"\n  [{:keys [identity deposit bind-host bind-port max-bytes]\n    :or {bind-host \"0.0.0.0\" bind-port 0}}]\n  (let [max-bytes (long (or max-bytes datagram/default-max-bytes))\n        running (atom true)\n        closed-deposited (atom false)\n        deposit-event!\n        (fn [event]\n          (try (stream/append! deposit event)\n               (catch Exception _ nil)))\n        socket\n        (try\n          (DatagramSocket.\n            (InetSocketAddress.\n              ^InetAddress (InetAddress/getByName ^String bind-host)\n              (int bind-port)))\n          (catch Exception e\n            (deposit-event! (datagram/bind-failed-event\n                              identity (str e)))\n            nil))]\n    (when socket\n      (deposit-event!\n        (datagram/bound-event identity\n                              (.getHostAddress (.getLocalAddress socket))\n                              (.getLocalPort socket)))\n      (let [thread\n            (Thread.\n              (fn []\n                (let [buf (byte-array 65536)\n                      packet (DatagramPacket. buf (alength buf))]\n                  (while @running\n                    ;; .receive leaves the packet's length at what the last\n                    ;; datagram filled; reset it to the buffer's own length\n                    ;; so a datagram after a short one is not truncated.\n                    (.setLength packet (alength buf))\n                    (when (try (.receive socket packet)\n                               true\n                               (catch Exception _\n                                 (when @running (Thread/sleep 10))\n(ns dao.stream.waitset.driver\n  \"Host policy — the JVM wake source for a wait-set owner.\n\n   This namespace is sleep machinery and nothing else. It holds no\n   interpreter state — no waitset, no store, no results, no cadence — and\n   dispatches nothing. The owner is an ordinary `loop` on one thread with\n   the waitset, the store, and the cadence state as loop locals:\n\n     check → consume :woken → cadence-step → sleep! → recur\n\n   A wake source is a `LinkedBlockingQueue` carrying contentless wake\n   tokens only: `nudge!` offers one, and `sleep!` polls with the timeout and\n   drains any surplus, so repeated nudges coalesce into one wake. This is\n   `select` with the wakeup pipe; the sweep is the syscall.\"\n  (:import (java.util.concurrent LinkedBlockingQueue TimeUnit)))\n\n\n(defn make-wake\n  \"A wake source: a queue of contentless tokens (Booleans) and nothing\n   else. It is never handed a waitset, a store, or a result.\"\n  []\n  (LinkedBlockingQueue.))\n\n\n(defn nudge!\n  \"Offer one contentless wake token: ask the owner's current sleep to end\n   early. Never blocks, runs no owner code inline, and may be called from\n   any thread — a line producer, a signal handler, a composition that just\n   appended. Repeated nudges coalesce inside `sleep!`'s drain. A nudge when\n   the owner is not sleeping costs one token that the next `sleep!`\n   consumes immediately.\"\n  [wake]\n  (.offer ^LinkedBlockingQueue wake true)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn sleep!\n  \"Park up to `sleep-ms` for one wake token. Returns true when a nudge\n   arrived — draining every surplus token first, so however many nudges\n   landed, this is the one wake they coalesce into — and false when the\n   timeout elapsed. `sleep-ms` is the caller's cadence answer; this\n   function adds nothing to it and knows nothing about the round it ends.\"\n  [wake sleep-ms]\n  (if (.poll ^LinkedBlockingQueue wake (long sleep-ms) TimeUnit/MILLISECONDS)\n    (do (loop []\n          (when (.poll ^LinkedBlockingQueue wake)\n            (recur)))\n        true)\n(def ^:private hex-digits \"0123456789abcdef\")\n\n\n(defn- byte-ints\n  [bs]\n  #?(:cljd (vec bs)\n     :clj (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 0xff) bs)\n     :cljs (vec (js/Array.from bs))))\n\n\n(defn- ints->bytes\n  [ints]\n  #?(:cljd (typed/Uint8List.fromList ints)\n     :clj (byte-array (map unchecked-byte ints))\n     :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (clj->js ints))))\n\n\n(defn- byte-count\n  [bs]\n  #?(:cljd (.-length ^typed/Uint8List bs)\n     :clj (alength ^bytes bs)\n     :cljs (.-length bs)))\n\n\n(defn- zero-bytes\n  [n]\n  #?(:cljd (typed/Uint8List n)\n     :clj (byte-array n)\n     :cljs (js/Uint8Array. n)))\n\n\n  #?(:cljd datagram.host/bind!\n     :cljs datagram.host/bind!\n     :clj datagram.host/bind!))\n\n\n(defn secure-random-bytes\n  \"`n` bytes from the host's cryptographically secure generator.\"\n  [n]\n  #?(:cljd (let [r (math/Random.secure)]\n             (typed/Uint8List.fromList\n               (vec (repeatedly n #(.nextInt r 256)))))\n     :clj (let [bs (byte-array n)]\n            (.nextBytes (java.security.SecureRandom.) bs)\n            bs)\n     :cljs (.getRandomValues js/crypto (js/Uint8Array. n))))\n\n\n(defn- refused\n(defn accept-connection!\n  \"Bounded upgrade callback entry.  An exhausted slot pool is closed\n   immediately; otherwise this deposits precisely one host-local offer and\n   waits for `endpoint-step` to consume a matching acknowledgement.  There\n   is no served-path lookup here any more: every upgrade this endpoint's\n   own codec negotiation accepts is accepted, and what a client can reach\n   through it is entirely the mirror's own table, answered per identity\n   after the connection opens.  `path` is the caller's own routing concern\n   (a host bound to more than one endpoint still dispatches an upgrade to\n   the right one by it); this transport no longer inspects it.\n\n   The socket seam may carry `:ws/subprotocol`, the subprotocol the host\n   negotiated for this upgrade.  An endpoint serves every profile it was\n   composed with, concurrently; a named subprotocol it does not speak is a\n   refused handshake, never a downgrade, and an absent name is the Transit\n   default for sockets that negotiated nothing (direct composition and\n   tests).\"\n  ([endpoint path socket] (accept-connection! endpoint path socket nil))\n  ([endpoint _path socket now]\n   (let [descriptor (:descriptor (endpoint-state endpoint))\n         offered (:ws/subprotocol socket)\n         codec (if (nil? offered)\n                 transit/profile\n                 (get (:codec-index endpoint) offered))]\n     (cond\n       (nil? codec)\n       (do (invoke-close! socket protocol-close-code \"dao.stream/subprotocol-unsupported\")\n           {:ws/status :ws/unsupported-subprotocol})\n\n       :else\n       (let [state (:state endpoint)\n             chosen (volatile! nil)]\n(ns dao.jing.cbor.boring\n  \"The Boring half of the `dao.jing.cbor` codec (JVM and ClojureScript).\n\n   Boring is Jing's canonical CBOR writer. `dao.jing.cbor` never hands it a\n   Clojure value to interpret: it hands it a wire tree whose every node\n   already has exactly one CBOR meaning, so no Boring value mapping decides\n   a Jing byte:\n\n   * nil, booleans, strings, and host byte arrays;\n   * exact integers inside the 64-bit CBOR range (major types 0/1);\n   * vectors (CBOR arrays) and maps (CBOR maps, whose keys Boring's\n     `:canonical` profile sorts bytewise);\n   * `tagged` nodes: `boring.data/TaggedValue`, written as the tag number\n     over its content with no interpretation (tags 2, 3, 4, 30, 258);\n   * `frame` nodes: tagged literals, written as tag 27 over\n     `[name-string payload]` (Jing's four names and `clojure/with-meta`).\n\n   Metadata never rides on a wire node: Boring's own metadata mapping would\n   emit tag-39 keywords, so metadata is always an explicit\n   `clojure/with-meta` frame.\n\n   Encoding uses the ordinary one-item `boring.core/encode`, never the\n   indexed API, under `:canonical` with string references and shapes off.\n   Boring 0.1.30 locks those two fields under `:canonical` and throws on a\n   conflicting override, which the tests pin.\n\n   Decoding does not go through Boring: see `dao.jing.cbor`.\n\n   ClojureDart compiles every namespace on the source path and Boring has no\n   Dart half, so every form is `:cljd nil`-gated; J2 supplies Dart.\"\n  (:require\n    #?@(:cljd []\n        :default [[boring.core :as boring]\n                  [boring.data :as boring-data]])))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :default\n   (def opts\n     \"The one encode option map every call in this namespace uses.\"\n     {:profile :canonical :stringref false :shapes false}))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :default\n   (defn byte-payload?\n     \"True for a host byte array: byte[] on the JVM, Uint8Array on JS.\"\n     [x]\n     #?(:clj (bytes? x)\n        :cljs (instance? js/Uint8Array x))))\n\n\n;; Scalar and container nodes. Boring writes these host values with their\n;; one CBOR meaning, so each constructor is the identity (or builds the plain\n;; host collection); `dao.jing.cbor.cljd` implements the same vocabulary as\n;; `cbor` package objects.\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :default\n   (defn null\n     []\n     nil))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :default\n   (defn bool\n     [b]\n(ns dao.jing.cbor.cljd\n  \"The Dart half of the `dao.jing.cbor` byte writer, over the pinned pub.dev\n   `cbor` package (6.5.1). The twin of `dao.jing.cbor.boring`: the same wire\n   vocabulary, so `dao.jing.cbor`'s normalization is shared and only the\n   node constructors differ.\n\n   `dao.jing.cbor` builds the wire tree through these functions; each node\n   has exactly one CBOR meaning and the package writes it:\n\n   * `null`, `bool`, `text`, `byte-string`, `integer` (a Dart int or a\n     BigInt inside the 64-bit CBOR range; the package's CborInt writes the\n     shortest head, 8-byte arguments included);\n   * `array-node` over a Clojure vector of nodes; `map-node` over\n     [key-hex key-node value-node] triples, written in bytewise key order\n     (the package writes map entries in insertion order, so the order is\n     chosen here, not by the package);\n   * `tagged` (tags 2, 3, 4, 30, 258) over a vector of nodes or host\n     bytes; `frame` (tag 27 over [name-string payload]).\n\n   Decoding does not go through the package: `dao.jing.cbor`'s structural\n   reader is shared by every host.\"\n  (:require [\"package:cbor/cbor.dart\" :as cbor])\n  (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List]))\n\n\n(defn byte-payload?\n  \"True for the host byte array: Uint8List.\"\n  [x]\n  #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unresolved-namespace]}\n  (dart/is? x Uint8List))\n\n\n(defn null\n  []\n  (cbor/CborNull.))\n\n\n(defn bool\n  [b]\n  (cbor/CborBool. (true? b)))\n\n\n(defn text\n  [s]\n  (cbor/CborString. s))\n\n\n(defn byte-string\n  [bs]\n  (cbor/CborBytes. bs))\n\n\n(defn integer\n  \"A Dart int, or a BigInt inside [-2^64, 2^64).\"\n  [n]\n  (if (dart/is? n int)\n    (cbor/CborSmallInt. n)\n    (cbor/CborInt. n)))\n\n\n(defn array-node\n  [nodes]\n  (cbor/CborList. (vec nodes) .type cbor/CborLengthType.definite))\n\n\n(defn map-node\n  \"A CBOR map from [key-hex key-node value-node] triples, written with keys\n   in bytewise order of their canonical bytes (lowercase hex compares the\n   same way, a proper prefix first).\"\n  [wired]\n  (let [sorted (sort-by first wired)]\n    (cbor/CborMap.fromIterables\n      (mapv second sorted)\n      (mapv #(nth % 2) sorted)\n      .type cbor/CborLengthType.definite)))\n\n\n(defn- content-node\n  [content]\n  (if (byte-payload? content)\n    (cbor/CborBytes. content)\n    (array-node content)))\n\n\n(defn tagged\n  \"tag over content: host bytes (tags 2 and 3) or a vector of nodes.\"\n  [tag content]\n  (if (byte-payload? content)\n    (cbor/CborBytes. content .tags [tag])\n    (cbor/CborList. (vec content) .tags [tag] .type cbor/CborLengthType.definite)))\n\n\n(defn frame\n  \"Tag 27 over [name payload]; payload is host bytes (the float64 frame) or\n   a vector of nodes.\"\n  [tag-symbol payload]\n  (cbor/CborList.\n    [(cbor/CborString. (str tag-symbol)) (content-node payload)]\n    .tags [27]\n    .type cbor/CborLengthType.definite))\n\n\n(defn encode\n  \"Canonical CBOR bytes of one wire tree.\"\n  [wire]\n  #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unresolved-symbol]}\n  (Uint8List.fromList (cbor/cborEncode wire)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
